    ('378fe0dc-2d2d-4d91-abcf-4955b7c45230'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Raised on the citadel mound of Mohenjo-daro in the third millennium BCE, the Great Bath measures approximately 12 metres by 7 metres, with a maximum depth of 2.4 metres.', 'The earliest known public water tank, built in the 3rd millennium BC. It measures 12 by 7 metres and was waterproofed with bitumen — remarkably advanced engineering for its era.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:378fe0dc-2d2d-4d91-abcf-4955b7c45230:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The earliest known public water tank, built in the 3rd millennium BC. It measures 12 by 7 metres and was waterproofed with bitumen — remarkably advanced engineering for its era.'' restored on Great Bath', '41c03f4f3773cdb702312167d6197753c4d54ab291e714926b5fb0e905594a61|9a2e733c544a36f482ee3228120e2bb376ca6cc8750bb2c8e2b8e659bd1b4606|9a2e733c544a36f482ee3228120e2bb376ca6cc8750bb2c8e2b8e659bd1b4606', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Raised on the citadel mound of Mohenjo-daro in the third millennium BCE, the Great Bath measures approximately 12 metres by 7 metres, with a maximum depth of 2.4 metres."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Great Bath is on the citadel mound of Mohenjo-daro [S1, S2]; it was built in the third millennium BCE, after the citadel mound was raised [S2]; it measures approximately 12 metres by 7 metres [S4]; its maximum depth is 2.4 metres [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-002 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Great Bath stands on the citadel mound of Mohenjo-daro [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Bath]; It was built in the third millennium BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Bath]; It measures approximately 12 metres by 7 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Bath]; Its maximum depth is 2.4 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Bath]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('37d8c068-dfb3-4fc5-a99e-a09c4aad18d3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the Andes, on the northern slope of Susupillo, a mountain about 4,400 metres high, lies an archaeological site of the same name, more than 3,800 metres up in the Huánuco Region.', 'An archaeological site at over 3,800 m in the Andes. Living this high forced adaptation to extreme cold, thin air, and limited growing seasons.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:37d8c068-dfb3-4fc5-a99e-a09c4aad18d3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''An archaeological site at over 3,800 m in the Andes. Living this high forced adaptation to extreme cold, thin air, and limited growing seasons.'' restored on Susupillo', '78deadb1fc4d540f323b7ad901bb53bae1ae06d9c0e9ad6c061549b5328ac927|07be0c082487d3df31716cb4ed35677a5f386c2cd303dca3d3eee800f28999f1|07be0c082487d3df31716cb4ed35677a5f386c2cd303dca3d3eee800f28999f1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Andes, on the northern slope of Susupillo, a mountain about 4,400 metres high, lies an archaeological site of the same name, more than 3,800 metres up in the Huánuco Region."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Susupillo is in the Andes [S1]; Susupillo is a mountain about 4,400 metres high [S1]; an archaeological site of the same name lies on the mountain [S1]; the site lies on the northern slope [S3]; the site is more than 3,800 metres up [S3]; it is in the Huánuco Region [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-021 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Susupillo is an archaeological site, named like the mountain on whose slope it lies [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susupillo]; The mountain and site are in the Andes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susupillo]; The site lies in the Huánuco Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susupillo]; The mountain Susupillo is about 4,400 metres high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susupillo]; The site lies on the northern slope of the mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susupillo]; The site lies more than 3,800 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susupillo]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('386cadca-23fc-463a-ba46-c016494086ba'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On an artificially modified karstic hill, the acropolis of Ixtonton spreads around two plazas. By the time the site was first reported in 1985, most of its stelae had already been looted.', 'Ixtonton was the capital city of one of the four Maya kingdoms in the upper Mopan Valley. The acropolis at Ixtonton is laid out around two plazas on top of an artificially modified karstic hill.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:386cadca-23fc-463a-ba46-c016494086ba:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Ixtonton was the capital city of one of the four Maya kingdoms in the upper Mopan Valley. The acropolis at Ixtonton is laid out around two plazas on top of an artificially modified karstic hill.'' restored on Ixtonton', '652db538eb47f10803ad6c200546537a6e311064fdc0092a71023dcde1b047a5|67bcaafd3dba426311afbd44ada803bf9a34efff5e37813e52f14c7b988b4118|67bcaafd3dba426311afbd44ada803bf9a34efff5e37813e52f14c7b988b4118', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On an artificially modified karstic hill, the acropolis of Ixtonton spreads around two plazas. By the time the site was first reported in 1985, most of its stelae had already been looted."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the acropolis of Ixtonton is on an artificially modified karstic hill [S5]; the acropolis is laid out around two plazas [S5]; the site was first reported in 1985 [S6]; by then most of its stelae had already been looted [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-021 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ixtonton is an archaeological site (with an acropolis) in Guatemala [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixtonton]; The acropolis of Ixtonton spreads around two plazas on an artificially modified karstic hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixtonton]; The site was first reported in 1985 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixtonton]; By the time the site was first reported, most of its stelae had already been looted [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixtonton]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('388a75e4-cdec-4123-b545-2eb6d9b0fa14'::uuid, 'card_description', 'First occupied at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC, the Castro of Zambujal kept oval adobe houses approximately 6 metres in diameter behind a wall built to protect it from attack.', 'The site was first occupied in the Chalcolithic period, at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:388a75e4-cdec-4123-b545-2eb6d9b0fa14:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The site was first occupied in the Chalcolithic period, at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC.'' restored on Castro of Zambujal', '20b9494c9a9d14afe221c2459f8ecdc9d8ef2e5e92e5ed74538e6817adba265b|9ea40d2bb536d48c2f05d3d4940ea4047cfa4024831a57127215642f17a8ef0c|9ea40d2bb536d48c2f05d3d4940ea4047cfa4024831a57127215642f17a8ef0c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "First occupied at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC, the Castro of Zambujal kept oval adobe houses approximately 6 metres in diameter behind a wall built to protect it from attack."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site was first occupied at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC [S3]; its houses were oval [S5]; the houses were made of adobe [S5]; the houses were approximately 6 metres in diameter [S5]; the settlement had a wall around it [S4]; the wall was built to protect it from attack [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-021 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Castro of Zambujal is a fortified Chalcolithic settlement in Portugal (Torres Vedras) [SUPPORTED: https://patrimonios.pt/en/castro-do-zambujal/]; The Castro of Zambujal was first occupied at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Zambujal]; Its houses were oval [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Zambujal]; Its houses were approximately 6 metres in diameter [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Zambujal]; Its houses were made of adobe [SUPPORTED: https://patrimonios.pt/en/castro-do-zambujal/]; The oval adobe houses stood behind (within the enclosure of) the walls [SUPPORTED: https://patrimonios.pt/en/castro-do-zambujal/]; The settlement built a wall around itself to protect its community from attack [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Zambujal]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Passing an infant through one of its natural holes was believed to cure measles and whooping cough: the Longstone of Minchinhampton, a large piece of oolitic limestone 7.5 feet high.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description None restored on Longstone of Minchinhampton', '7cb52e2fbb5acf2b0c9712540a66dad15f5f5ddc26b8fd4fd60474f83b3b5e28|6868807378509a8e4ad357ba1d6a52652b0da5631eb9f515de84b343f1c6da34|6868807378509a8e4ad357ba1d6a52652b0da5631eb9f515de84b343f1c6da34', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Passing an infant through one of its natural holes was believed to cure measles and whooping cough: the Longstone of Minchinhampton, a large piece of oolitic limestone 7.5 feet high."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the stone has natural holes [S3]; passing a child through one of the holes was believed to cure infants of measles and whooping cough (hedged as belief) [S6]; the Longstone of Minchinhampton is a large piece of oolitic limestone [S2]; it is 7.5 feet high [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-021 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Longstone of Minchinhampton is a standing stone in Minchinhampton, Gloucestershire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longstone_of_Minchinhampton]; Passing an infant through one of its holes was believed to cure measles and whooping cough [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longstone_of_Minchinhampton]; The stone has natural holes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longstone_of_Minchinhampton]; The Longstone is a large piece of oolitic limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longstone_of_Minchinhampton]; The Longstone is 7.5 feet high [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/longstone-of-minchinhampton]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('398c3ef8-56c3-4640-a28a-ebaa8a36df33'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Banks reinforce the downland spur of Ebsbury, a possible hill fort. Buried in two earthenware pots lay Roman coins from between AD 337 and AD 408, found with six silver rings.', 'A downland spur in Wiltshire holds an enclosed Iron Age settlement and field system; the outer enclosure measures 700 by 400 metres. Roman-era layers sit above, preserving two superimposed cultures.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:398c3ef8-56c3-4640-a28a-ebaa8a36df33:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A downland spur in Wiltshire holds an enclosed Iron Age settlement and field system; the outer enclosure measures 700 by 400 metres. Roman-era layers sit above, preserving two superimposed cultures.'' restored on Ebsbury', 'e7b1860dfa6f1f986b3d1f7d131557d62d15801a51d7d3b83da2f40a56428548|3a4ff320730f95d239b88b9ed12f31cf9ff3b9e455d8ecdf1a183aaf08402301|3a4ff320730f95d239b88b9ed12f31cf9ff3b9e455d8ecdf1a183aaf08402301', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Banks reinforce the downland spur of Ebsbury, a possible hill fort. Buried in two earthenware pots lay Roman coins from between AD 337 and AD 408, found with six silver rings."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "banks reinforce the hill at Ebsbury [S3]; Ebsbury occupies a downland spur [S2]; Ebsbury is a possible hill fort [S1, S2]; Roman coins were found buried in two earthenware pots [S6, S7]; the coins date from between AD 337 and AD 408 [S7]; the coins were found with six silver rings [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-022 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ebsbury is a possible hill fort (an Iron Age enclosed settlement, field system and possible hill fort) in Wiltshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebsbury]; Ebsbury occupies the spur of a downland hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebsbury]; Banks reinforce the hill (north and east sides) of the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebsbury]; Two Roman coin hoards were found at Ebsbury [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebsbury]; The coin hoards were buried in two earthenware pots [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebsbury]; The coins date from between AD 337 and AD 408 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebsbury]; The coins were found with six silver rings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebsbury]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3a8c5634-917e-401d-9eac-71d0363f2abf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Carved into Mount Starius near Antakya, the Church of St. Peter is a cave where water seeping from the rocks was gathered for baptism. St. Paul is believed to have been baptised here.', 'Carved 13 meters deep into Mount Starius near Antakya, this 4th-century cave church measures 9.5 meters wide and 7 meters high. It predates most standing cathedrals by several centuries.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3a8c5634-917e-401d-9eac-71d0363f2abf:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Carved 13 meters deep into Mount Starius near Antakya, this 4th-century cave church measures 9.5 meters wide and 7 meters high. It predates most standing cathedrals by several centuries.'' restored on Church of St. Peter', '949f3ae078e8fff4fb427f411877b01c5dcb2e651689cd0b1dcf660d79373594|bd55590760adb0372356dfc747bcbbc26a2db50e7ede829f10cbb489349d9ad9|bd55590760adb0372356dfc747bcbbc26a2db50e7ede829f10cbb489349d9ad9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Carved into Mount Starius near Antakya, the Church of St. Peter is a cave where water seeping from the rocks was gathered for baptism. St. Paul is believed to have been baptised here."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Church of St. Peter is carved into Mount Starius [S1]; it lies near Antakya [S1]; the church is a cave [S1]; water seeping from the rocks was gathered inside [S3]; the water was used for baptism [S3]; St. Paul is believed to have been baptised here [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-022 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Church of St. Peter is a cave (a church composed of a cave) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Saint_Peter]; The cave is carved into the mountainside on Mount Starius [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Saint_Peter]; The church is near Antakya (Antioch) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Saint_Peter]; Water seeps from the rocks near the cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Saint_Peter]; The seeping water was gathered inside the cave for baptism [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Saint_Peter]; St. Paul is believed to have been baptised at the church [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Saint_Peter]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3c56c4c5-f5a8-48bb-8b93-9e3a92feac09'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Strong Murus Gallicus battlements protected Bibracte, the Gallic oppidum and capital of the Aedui, where in 52 BC Vercingetorix was proclaimed head of the Gaulish coalition.', 'The capital of the Aedui tribe and one of Gaul''s most powerful hillforts. Julius Caesar defeated the Helvetii nearby in 58 BC, and Vercingetorix was proclaimed leader here.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3c56c4c5-f5a8-48bb-8b93-9e3a92feac09:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description "The capital of the Aedui tribe and one of Gaul''s most powerful hillforts. Julius Caesar defeated the Helvetii nearby in 58 BC, and Vercingetorix was proclaimed leader here." restored on Bibracte', 'b23cc61d17bc0621f35cfa803d310b59db4da5f1d60fcc9e79a7183cb6f437f9|6ba11ba493ae1e31163168f00b88afa7f7499039a5beea34a1205e17fd621945|6ba11ba493ae1e31163168f00b88afa7f7499039a5beea34a1205e17fd621945', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Strong Murus Gallicus battlements protected Bibracte, the Gallic oppidum and capital of the Aedui, where in 52 BC Vercingetorix was proclaimed head of the Gaulish coalition."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "strong Murus Gallicus battlements protected Bibracte [S7]; Bibracte was a Gallic oppidum [S1]; Bibracte was the capital of the Aedui [S1]; in 52 BC Vercingetorix was proclaimed head of the Gaulish coalition at Bibracte [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-022 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bibracte is a Gallic oppidum (fortified settlement) [SUPPORTED: https://e