    ('3cb81240-5b1e-4505-a73a-4c0d02fd439c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Among low ruined walls in County Kerry, a cross-slab standing stone sits in the middle of the Riasc Monastic Settlement, an early monastic site now a National Monument in state care.', 'An early monastic site with low stone walls and a cross-slab standing stone. Artefacts found here date the community to the early Christian era.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3cb81240-5b1e-4505-a73a-4c0d02fd439c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''An early monastic site with low stone walls and a cross-slab standing stone. Artefacts found here date the community to the early Christian era.'' restored on Riasc Monastic Settlement', 'dfe5f50f1799c09a8a5ac1aa29e6fcad0dff0dfb6fb528b3814121628193640b|cb7e4b97a7e2ee4af094cf14faf82f71e5df89755d3772ab458e452155a890fd|cb7e4b97a7e2ee4af094cf14faf82f71e5df89755d3772ab458e452155a890fd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Among low ruined walls in County Kerry, a cross-slab standing stone sits in the middle of the Riasc Monastic Settlement, an early monastic site now a National Monument in state care."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site has low ruined walls [S1, S2]; the site is in County Kerry [S1]; a cross-slab standing stone sits in the middle of the compound [S2]; it is an early monastic site [S1]; it is a National Monument in state care [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-022 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Riasc (Reask) is an early monastic site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reask]; Riasc is in County Kerry, Ireland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reask]; The site has low ruined walls (low remnants of buildings and enclosure walls) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reask]; A cross-slab standing stone sits in the middle of the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reask]; The site is a National Monument [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reask]; The site is in state care (care of the Office of Public Works) [SUPPORTED: https://heritageireland.ie/unguided-sites/reask-ecclesiastical-site/]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3ccc24a7-a41a-4c7b-91e2-246b6b81ef05'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a high plateau by the Rhine, Augusta Raurica had the largest Roman theatre north of the Alps, 8,000 to 10,000 seats, and exported smoked pork and bacon to other parts of the Empire.', 'A Roman city from 44 BC with the best-preserved theatre north of the Alps. A massive silver treasure of 270 items was found buried here, hidden during 3rd-century raids.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3ccc24a7-a41a-4c7b-91e2-246b6b81ef05:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A Roman city from 44 BC with the best-preserved theatre north of the Alps. A massive silver treasure of 270 items was found buried here, hidden during 3rd-century raids.'' restored on Augusta Raurica', 'c1b74944d79d4f0ab65e6a7d4bc40b4198e23da217e634671245232a23d3b4bd|677a020287cf3834e972d794fe9427496d4946902971b9324f145e3a19f001e6|677a020287cf3834e972d794fe9427496d4946902971b9324f145e3a19f001e6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a high plateau by the Rhine, Augusta Raurica had the largest Roman theatre north of the Alps, 8,000 to 10,000 seats, and exported smoked pork and bacon to other parts of the Empire."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Augusta Raurica lies on a high plateau [S4]; it lies by the Rhine [S1, S4]; it had the largest Roman theatre north of the Alps [S6]; the theatre had 8,000 to 10,000 seats [S6]; it exported smoked pork and bacon to other parts of the Roman Empire [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-022 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Augusta Raurica is a Roman archaeological site (Roman colony) on the Rhine in Switzerland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusta_Raurica]; Augusta Raurica was founded on a high plateau by the Rhine [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusta_Raurica]; Augusta Raurica had the largest Roman theatre north of the Alps [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusta_Raurica]; The theatre had 8,000 to 10,000 seats [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusta_Raurica]; Augusta Raurica exported smoked pork and bacon to other parts of the Roman Empire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusta_Raurica]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3d1c5bc3-f16b-4459-afd5-0d4034875aa2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A red deer antler pick from its ditch suggests the Stonehenge Cursus was first built between 3630 and 3375 BCE. Just under 3 km long, it is oriented toward the equinox sunrise.', 'A 3 km Neolithic earthwork from 3630–3375 BC—centuries before Stonehenge itself. It was the first monument built in this vast ritual landscape.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3d1c5bc3-f16b-4459-afd5-0d4034875aa2:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A 3 km Neolithic earthwork from 3630–3375 BC—centuries before Stonehenge itself. It was the first monument built in this vast ritual landscape.'' restored on Stonehenge Cursus', '2f5d430fef8191915a2fd903fb4bb36b3e5a1a8230aa252581c9a1979f926d0f|e575b25308e251b5c47c14ad18fd7123ea91de938adc54c6e09695af8884c0b6|e575b25308e251b5c47c14ad18fd7123ea91de938adc54c6e09695af8884c0b6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A red deer antler pick from its ditch suggests the Stonehenge Cursus was first built between 3630 and 3375 BCE. Just under 3 km long, it is oriented toward the equinox sunrise."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a red deer antler pick was found in its ditch [S2]; the pick suggests the Stonehenge Cursus was first built between 3630 and 3375 BCE [S2]; it is just under 3 km long [S3]; it is oriented toward the equinox sunrise [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-023 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Stonehenge Cursus is a Neolithic cursus monument (an earthwork enclosure) near Stonehenge in Wiltshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Cursus]; A red deer antler pick was found in the cursus ditch (at the bottom of the western terminal ditch) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Cursus]; The antler pick suggests the Stonehenge Cursus was first built between 3630 and 3375 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Cursus]; The cursus is just under 3 km long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Cursus]; The cursus is oriented toward the equinox sunrise [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Cursus]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3e25aa3a-9fb4-4e40-9989-07039d798515'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the small shelters of Agrour Amogjar, damaged rock paintings show giraffe, lion and crocodile, herds of cattle, handprints and sunburst circles, and a frieze of dancers.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3e25aa3a-9fb4-4e40-9989-07039d798515:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description None restored on Agrour Amogjar', '14bf1110e13035674714d7e34b2e75500d436f975f6c2cd3ca3e597ef4d6d16c|31703a29bdb0aa07d53311bdc2ff54bea927a0ad327f33563a9b3690c7f3397c|31703a29bdb0aa07d53311bdc2ff54bea927a0ad327f33563a9b3690c7f3397c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the small shelters of Agrour Amogjar, damaged rock paintings show giraffe, lion and crocodile, herds of cattle, handprints and sunburst circles, and a frieze of dancers."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Agrour Amogjar has small shelters [S2]; the shelters hold rock paintings in a damaged state [S2]; the paintings show giraffe, lion and crocodile [S5]; the paintings show herds of cattle [S5]; the paintings show handprints [S5]; the paintings show circles with sunburst design [S5]; there is a frieze of dancers [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-023 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Agrour Amogjar is a site of small shelters (on a peak near the Amogjar Pass, Adrar plateau, Mauritania) holding rock paintings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrour_Amogjar]; The rock paintings of Agrour Amogjar are damaged [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrour_Amogjar]; The paintings show giraffe, lion and crocodile [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrour_Amogjar]; The paintings show herds of cattle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrour_Amogjar]; The paintings include handprints [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrour_Amogjar]; The paintings include sunburst circles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrour_Amogjar]; The paintings include a frieze of dancers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrour_Amogjar]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3f5089f0-0b0d-45c8-908c-9155de71c6b3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a ridge overlooking the Valle de los Espejos, at about 2,850 meters, the temples of Huamango were once roofed with wooden beams under a thick clay sealing layer.', 'An early Postclassic city built on leveled terraces at 2,850 metres. Structures include a palace, an altar, and a wall, rich in ancestral Otomi traditions.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:3f5089f0-0b0d-45c8-908c-9155de71c6b3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''An early Postclassic city built on leveled terraces at 2,850 metres. Structures include a palace, an altar, and a wall, rich in ancestral Otomi traditions.'' restored on Huamango', '027b34ba0b9251f7675f0c8adbd792092f6de776515fabd847002b8e42480d2c|3922203ce2d985499e502aede221585fa66b160c919e11428f262fd9774bf343|3922203ce2d985499e502aede221585fa66b160c919e11428f262fd9774bf343', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a ridge overlooking the Valle de los Espejos, at about 2,850 meters, the temples of Huamango were once roofed with wooden beams under a thick clay sealing layer."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huamango''s temples lie on a ridge overlooking the Valle de los Espejos [S5]; the site lies at about 2,850 meters altitude [S2]; Huamango has temples [S5]; the temples were roofed with wooden beams [S6]; the roofs were covered with a thick clay sealing layer [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-023 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huamango is an archaeological site with temples (a ceremonial zone), in Mexico (State of Mexico) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huamango]; The temples stand on a ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huamango]; The ridge overlooks the Valle de los Espejos [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huamango]; The site lies at about 2,850 meters altitude [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huamango]; The temples were once roofed with a wooden beam structure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huamango]; The wooden beam roofs were covered with a thick clay sealing layer [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huamango]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('412a1f97-7dfd-4624-b6ec-2813217f39bf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Pločnik a 120 hectare Vinča settlement stood from 5500 BCE until fire destroyed it in 4700 BCE, its people sleeping on woollen mats and fur, its figurines showing daily life.', 'A 120-hectare Neolithic settlement of the Vinca culture, occupied from 5500 to 4700 BC. It was destroyed by a catastrophic fire and never rebuilt.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:412a1f97-7dfd-4624-b6ec-2813217f39bf:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A 120-hectare Neolithic settlement of the Vinca culture, occupied from 5500 to 4700 BC. It was destroyed by a catastrophic fire and never rebuilt.'' restored on Pločnik - Archaeological Site', 'a608c380ff3e69868ed7402903536b012504cb1e5a5781d1b1f42da394ac0172|3dd9cd1de1b1e198d0cebdb7e6eb20f30a384c0082e14410f3a9390de5c41c2c|3dd9cd1de1b1e198d0cebdb7e6eb20f30a384c0082e14410f3a9390de5c41c2c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Pločnik a 120 hectare Vinča settlement stood from 5500 BCE until fire destroyed it in 4700 BCE, its people sleeping on woollen mats and fur, its figurines showing daily life."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a settlement stood at Pločnik [S1, S2]; the settlement covered 120 hectares [S2]; it belonged to the Vinča culture [S2]; it existed from 5500 BCE [S2]; it was destroyed by fire in 4700 BCE [S2]; its people slept on woollen mats and fur [S4]; its figurines show daily life [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-023 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pločnik (Serbia) is an archaeological site with a Neolithic Vinča culture settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]; The Vinča settlement at Pločnik covered 120 hectares [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]; The settlement stood from 5500 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]; The settlement was destroyed by fire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]; The destruction by fire was in 4700 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]; Its people slept on woollen mats [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]; Its people slept on fur [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]; Its figurines show daily life [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plo%C4%8Dnik_(archaeological_site)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4154f90b-74f1-49d4-bdab-caa7bb1322fb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Rome built Noviodunum, Gaulish for new fortress, on the site of a Celtic temple. Its 3rd century fortifications remain the most impressive features of Jublains Archaeological Site.', 'A Gallo-Roman city built atop a Celtic temple, serving as the Diablintes'' capital. Its public buildings trace the spread of Roman urban planning into Gaul.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4154f90b-74f1-49d4-bdab-caa7bb1322fb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description "A Gallo-Roman city built atop a Celtic temple, serving as the Diablintes'' capital. Its public buildings trace the spread of Roman urban planning into Gaul." restored on Jublains Archaeological Site', '082eb13fbdbc4dacb431225587bf602059fa7a5a542ebcec62528cce5a45c9ea|570180e256b754d2e69b2696e9c86fac5f30583ae34cab43830ffab11044cec4|570180e256b754d2e69b2696e9c86fac5f30583ae34cab43830ffab11044cec4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Rome built Noviodunum, Gaulish for new fortress, on the site of a Celtic temple. Its 3rd century fortifications remain the most impressive features of Jublains Archaeological Site."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Rome built a city named Noviodunum [S2]; Noviodunum is Gaulish for new fortress [S5]; the city was built on the site of a Celtic temple [S2]; the city has fortifications from the 3rd century [S3]; these fortifications remain the most impressive features of the Jublains site [S3]; Noviodunum lies at the Jublains archaeological site [S1, S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-023 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Jublains Archaeological Site is a cluster of Roman ruins in the commune of Jublains, Mayenne, France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jublains_archeological_site]; Rome (the Roman imperial authorities) built the city of Noviodunum at Jublains [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jublains_archeological_site]; Noviodunum is Gaulish for new fortress [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jublains_archeological_site]; Noviodunum was built on the site of a Celtic temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jublains_archeological_site]; The fortifications at the site date to the 3rd century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jublains_archeological_site]; The 3rd century fortifications remain the most impressive features of the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jublains_archeological_site]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('416634af-9655-4973-a39a-9e1a9e3f0f2c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The granite Menhir of Patalou, 4 meters tall, dates back to the 5th millennium BCE and is believed to be the second oldest menhir identified in Western Europe. It was re-erected in 2015.', 'The menhir is a megalithic standing stone dating back to the 5th millennium BCE.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:416634af-9655-4973-a39a-9e1a9e3f0f2c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The menhir is a megalithic standing stone dating back to the 5th millennium BCE.'' restored on Menhir of Patalou', '44c056f58ff7881957f009b3b3319542b046251824c13f2e0406374fa920d0c1|62301bb91165028ea11130366ece940afe450a2f1e7fa631b1b9dca53314d2f6|62301bb91165028ea11130366ece940afe450a2f1e7fa631b1b9dca53314d2f6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The granite Menhir of Patalou, 4 meters tall, dates back to the 5th millennium BCE and is believed to be the second oldest menhir identified in Western Europe. It was re-erected in 2015."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Menhir of Patalou is made of granite [S6]; it is 4 meters tall [S7]; it dates back to the 5th millennium BCE [S2]; it is believed to be the second oldest menhir identified in Western Europe [S3]; it was re-erected in 2015 [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-024 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Menhir of Patalou is a megalithic standing stone (menhir) at Nisa, Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Patalou]; The menhir is made of granite [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://www.cm-nisa.pt/index.php/imagem/noticias/353-noticias-2023/3622-menir-do-patalou-classificado-monumento-de-interesse-publico]; The menhir is 4 meters tall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Patalou]; The menhir dates back to the 5th millennium BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Patalou]; It is believed to be the second oldest menhir identified in Western Europe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Patalou]; The menhir was re-erected in 2015 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Patalou]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('41701276-d1e1-4068-b767-5b6173f6512c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Illegal excavations in 1992 exposed Minoan buildings on Crete: the Galatas Palace, whose large paved central courtyard measured 16 meters by 32 meters. Its east wing is best preserved.', 'Galatas was discovered in 1992 when illegal excavations revealed the existence of several buildings associated with the Minoans.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:41701276-d1e1-4068-b767-5b6173f6512c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Galatas was discovered in 1992 when illegal excavations revealed the existence of several buildings associated with the Minoans.'' restored on Galatas Palace', 'e73008bf9f89e2d8ebcd811fb27fc62626999b5edcf5281931785baea6854107|ebfcb432b106741ba97d2536e2229fa7460b47d697245824851dc01ea56573e1|ebfcb432b106741ba97d2536e2229fa7460b47d697245824851dc01ea56573e1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Illegal excavations in 1992 exposed Minoan buildings on Crete: the Galatas Palace, whose large paved central courtyard measured 16 meters by 32 meters. Its east wing is best preserved."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "illegal excavations in 1992 revealed Minoan buildings [S4]; the site is on Crete [S1]; the Galatas Palace was discovered through these excavations [S4]; the palace has a large paved central courtyard [S5]; the courtyard measured 16 meters by 32 meters [S5]; its east wing is the best-preserved part [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-024 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Galatas Palace is a Minoan palace site on Crete, Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galatas_Palace]; The site was exposed by illegal excavations [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galatas_Palace]; The illegal excavations took place in 1992 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galatas_Palace]; The illegal excavations exposed Minoan buildings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galatas_Palace]; The palace has a large paved central courtyard [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galatas_Palace]; The central courtyard measured 16 meters by 32 meters [SUPPORTED: http://www.minoancrete.com/galatas.htm]; The east wing of the palace is the best preserved [SUPPORTED: http://www.minoancrete.com/galatas.htm]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('41bb2c5e-4eb3-4bdf-8d1e-6ea497c44b26'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Above the Liptovská Mara reservoir, the wooden shrine of Havránok rose around an exceptionally high column. A pit nearby held bones of at least seven people sacrificed in druidic rituals.', 'The Havránok hill fort was an important religious, economic, and political center of the Púchov culture.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:41bb2c5e-4eb3-4bdf-8d1e-6ea497c44b26:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The Havránok hill fort was an important religious, economic, and political center of the Púchov culture.'' restored on Havránok', '19626f55afeb0e10a83bd3d9e859a3e91649765f581b9739c28dcd9edff1a65f|aae0909c67c513ef1f1c6d767e307428b20a8a4a57393f4375f6396afc49c6d2|aae0909c67c513ef1f1c6d767e307428b20a8a4a57393f4375f6396afc49c6d2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Above the Liptovská Mara reservoir, the wooden shrine of Havránok rose around an exceptionally high column. A pit nearby held bones of at least seven people sacrificed in druidic rituals."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Havránok lies above the Liptovská Mara reservoir [S2]; Havránok had a wooden shrine [S5]; the shrine was built around an exceptionally high column [S5]; a pit near the central cult object held bones of at least seven people [S6]; these people were sacrificed during druidic rituals [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-024 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Havránok is an archaeological site (Celtic hill fort with a shrine) on a hill above the Liptovská Mara reservoir in Slovakia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Havr%C3%A1nok]; Its wooden shrine was built around an exceptionally high column [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Havr%C3%A1nok]; A pit near the shrine held bones of at least seven people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Havr%C3%A1nok]; The seven people were sacrificed in druidic rituals [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Havr%C3%A1nok]; Havránok had a cult place of Druids [SUPPORTED: https://slovakia.travel/en/havranok]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('43e8118f-ea04-4525-88a6-ed50a41bd240'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Across the Guadiana River at Mérida, the Puente Romano runs 60 spans over 721 m, its piers rounded upstream to withstand the current. A Visigoth, Sala, restored it in 686.', 'Puente Romano has undergone at least two significant restorations: once by Sala, a Visigoth, in 686 and once by Phillip II in 1610.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:43e8118f-ea04-4525-88a6-ed50a41bd240:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Puente Romano has undergone at least two significant restorations: once by Sala, a Visigoth, in 686 and once by Phillip II in 1610.'' restored on Puente Romano, Mérida', 'aff2be7a4f46ed7da4d5b1e2d88f6cfe02028e4b093c20f1a0c5f9d598daef46|633f1d9a1b8f69153223a1b6153c0e479f5b747d703e2635f7165f0e10f8e2bd|633f1d9a1b8f69153223a1b6153c0e479f5b747d703e2635f7165f0e10f8e2bd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Across the Guadiana River at Mérida, the Puente Romano runs 60 spans over 721 m, its piers rounded upstream to withstand the current. A Visigoth, Sala, restored it in 686."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Puente Romano crosses the Guadiana River [S1]; it is at Mérida [S1]; it has 60 spans [S4]; the spans run over 721 m [S4]; its piers are rounded on the upstream side to withstand the current [S2]; Sala, a Visigoth, restored it in 686 [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-024 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Puente Romano is a Roman bridge at Mérida, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_Romano,_M%C3%A9rida]; The bridge crosses the Guadiana River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_Romano,_M%C3%A9rida]; The bridge has 60 spans [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_Romano,_M%C3%A9rida]; The 60 spans run over a length of 721 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_Romano,_M%C3%A9rida]; The piers are rounded on the upstream side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_Romano,_M%C3%A9rida]; The rounded piers are designed to withstand the river current [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_Romano,_M%C3%A9rida]; A Visigoth named Sala restored the bridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_Romano,_M%C3%A9rida]; The restoration took place in 686 [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://www.britannica.com/place/Merida-Spain]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('446c8f5c-fbaa-4344-ae81-4b59d21f5c1a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Cardiff, the large capstone of Tinkinswood rests on smaller upright stones. Built around 4000 BC, the chamber held 920 human bones when excavated in 1914, nearly all broken.', 'The site was excavated in 1914, and inside the chamber there were 920 human bones, which were nearly all broken.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:446c8f5c-fbaa-4344-ae81-4b59d21f5c1a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The site was excavated in 1914, and inside the chamber there were 920 human bones, which were nearly all broken.'' restored on Tinkinswood', 'e2f678c9a714e32d1f55b31104925651ca70f823c4966cf187d62639aef0620f|a8d9a562198ce65539cfd883132fb92362554396428100d032f774714b0578f1|a8d9a562198ce65539cfd883132fb92362554396428100d032f774714b0578f1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-008", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Cardiff, the large capstone of Tinkinswood rests on smaller upright stones. Built around 4000 BC, the chamber held 920 human bones when excavated in 1914, nearly all broken."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-008 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tinkinswood is near Cardiff [S1]; it has a large capstone [S3]; the capstone rests on smaller upright stones [S3]; it was built around 4000 BC [S1]; the chamber held 920 human bones [S4]; it was excavated in 1914 [S4]; the bones were nearly all broken [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-024 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tinkinswood is a megalithic burial chamber in Wales [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tinkinswood]; Tinkinswood is near Cardiff [SUPPORTED: