    ('44c520ac-00bf-4bb6-a11a-8875c559b10e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Until 1927 Leahill Turret lay buried beneath the road near Banks. Built shortly after AD 122 on Hadrian''s Wall, it was later dismantled, and a skeleton was uncovered in the 1958 excavations.', 'A small lookout turret built on Hadrian''s Wall shortly after 122 AD. It was dismantled under Septimius Severus and later rebuilt using reclaimed stone.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:44c520ac-00bf-4bb6-a11a-8875c559b10e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description "A small lookout turret built on Hadrian''s Wall shortly after 122 AD. It was dismantled under Septimius Severus and later rebuilt using reclaimed stone." restored on Leahill Turret, Hadrian''s Wall', '886f72a41f27aa9cfc393498633a33c8bb34360dd1ebd367f442cfab4bef2af8|816033f3537f84d1045df6beb49c0a594f936a956b6c01cb703fff19b69d6055|816033f3537f84d1045df6beb49c0a594f936a956b6c01cb703fff19b69d6055', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Until 1927 Leahill Turret lay buried beneath the road near Banks. Built shortly after AD 122 on Hadrian''s Wall, it was later dismantled, and a skeleton was uncovered in the 1958 excavations."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "until 1927 Leahill Turret lay buried beneath the road [S5]; it lies near Banks [S1]; it was built shortly after AD 122 [S3]; it was built on / as part of Hadrian''s Wall [S1, S3]; it was later dismantled [S3]; a skeleton was uncovered during the 1958 excavations [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-verify-025 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Leahill Turret is a turret (lookout tower) of Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leahill_Turret,_Hadrian%27s_Wall]; The turret is located near Banks (England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leahill_Turret,_Hadrian%27s_Wall]; Until 1927 the turret lay buried beneath the road [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leahill_Turret,_Hadrian%27s_Wall]; It was built shortly after AD 122 as part of Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leahill_Turret,_Hadrian%27s_Wall]; It was later dismantled [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leahill_Turret,_Hadrian%27s_Wall]; A skeleton was uncovered in the 1958 excavations [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leahill_Turret,_Hadrian%27s_Wall]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('45958d56-72b2-44e3-ada0-c7030f469445'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Stylized dolphins, cups and leaves hide in the complicated geometry of the Lopen Roman Mosaic, almost 7 metres square and laid in Blue Lias stone from the surrounding Somerset hills.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:45958d56-72b2-44e3-ada0-c7030f469445:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description None restored on Lopen Roman Mosaic', '84405fae2f4d8741e6c330a5f9d85421af989e36b825cac9259a17a7b0d9df37|c3d0df6e6620b889c73c935d78114cbc029032484d5a0717b08bdc510a319005|c3d0df6e6620b889c73c935d78114cbc029032484d5a0717b08bdc510a319005', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Stylized dolphins, cups and leaves hide in the complicated geometry of the Lopen Roman Mosaic, almost 7 metres square and laid in Blue Lias stone from the surrounding Somerset hills."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the mosaic has a complicated geometrical design [S5]; it includes stylized figurative elements: dolphins, cups and leaves [S5]; the (main) mosaic is almost 7 metres square [S5]; it is made of Blue Lias stone [S3]; the stone came from the surrounding hills [S3]; the site is in Somerset [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-verify-025 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Lopen Roman Mosaic is a Roman mosaic in the village of Lopen, Somerset, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lopen_Roman_Mosaic]; Its design is complicated geometry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lopen_Roman_Mosaic]; The design includes stylized dolphins, cups and leaves [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lopen_Roman_Mosaic]; The main mosaic is almost 7 metres square [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lopen_Roman_Mosaic]; It is laid in Blue Lias stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lopen_Roman_Mosaic]; The stone came from the surrounding hills (of Somerset) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lopen_Roman_Mosaic]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('45c83a06-69a6-46f3-bb7a-eea482b9767c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Giants battle the Olympian gods in high relief around the base of the Pergamon Altar, built on the acropolis of Pergamon in the 2nd century BC. Its relief panels were later taken to Berlin.', 'Remains of a monumental altar built during the reign of King Eumenes II in the 2nd century BC. Its sculpted frieze depicting the Gigantomachy is a masterwork of Hellenistic art.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:45c83a06-69a6-46f3-bb7a-eea482b9767c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Remains of a monumental altar built during the reign of King Eumenes II in the 2nd century BC. Its sculpted frieze depicting the Gigantomachy is a masterwork of Hellenistic art.'' restored on Pergamon Altar', '6ca4d6cbbe98b0510780b4c62c7f76243e3c4ebb9f8a1d0f1c11e1e35d8d99b9|40a33477e25a57210a71e234f145333f3a3708dfe9d499588b93436fd4403ff5|40a33477e25a57210a71e234f145333f3a3708dfe9d499588b93436fd4403ff5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Giants battle the Olympian gods in high relief around the base of the Pergamon Altar, built on the acropolis of Pergamon in the 2nd century BC. Its relief panels were later taken to Berlin."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the base of the altar carries a frieze in high relief [S3]; the relief shows Giants battling the Olympian gods [S3]; the altar was built on the acropolis of Pergamon [S1]; it was built in the 2nd century BC [S1]; its relief panels were later taken to Berlin [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-verify-025 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Pergamon Altar is a monumental altar built on the acropolis of Pergamon (modern Turkey) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pergamon_Altar]; It was built in the 2nd century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pergamon_Altar]; Giants battle the Olympian gods in the relief (the Gigantomachy) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pergamon_Altar]; The battle is shown in high relief around the base of the altar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pergamon_Altar]; The relief panels were later taken to Berlin [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pergamon_Altar]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('45fcb351-64fb-488a-ab19-35741221bf81'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Sahara''s fringe lies Gemellae Fortress Roman, a rectangular fort with walls mostly about 3 metres thick, where a cohort from Syria honoured Hadrian with a statue in about 126 AD.', 'The earliest epigraph retrieved from the site is an inscription for a statue of Emperor Hadrian, in about the year 126 AD.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:45fcb351-64fb-488a-ab19-35741221bf81:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The earliest epigraph retrieved from the site is an inscription for a statue of Emperor Hadrian, in about the year 126 AD.'' restored on Gemellae Fortress Roman', 'f39be16a380fc6540697f7dad889f8e228cb4b172dafcf87dbb310909ef86175|727225b6cd5d43bf50880d641f366a61682906325a53f8f5e289b9e6d77be243|727225b6cd5d43bf50880d641f366a61682906325a53f8f5e289b9e6d77be243', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Sahara''s fringe lies Gemellae Fortress Roman, a rectangular fort with walls mostly about 3 metres thick, where a cohort from Syria honoured Hadrian with a statue in about 126 AD."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies on the fringe of the Sahara [S1]; it is a fort [S1]; the fort is rectangular [S6]; its walls were for the most part about 3 metres thick [S8]; a cohort from Syria honoured Hadrian with a statue [S4]; this happened in about 126 AD [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-verify-025 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gemellae is a Roman fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemellae]; It lies on the fringe of the Sahara in Algeria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemellae]; The fort is rectangular [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemellae]; Its walls were mostly about 3 metres thick [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemellae]; The unit that honoured Hadrian was a cohort from Syria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemellae]; The cohort honoured Emperor Hadrian with a statue [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemellae]; The statue dates to about 126 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemellae]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('469a11d9-f643-4a16-a4d5-bd02ffa78850'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An election notice on its facade, backing Gaius Sallustius for office, named the House of Sallust, among the most sumptuous homes of Pompeii, its oldest parts dated to the 4th century BCE.', 'One of Pompeii''s oldest houses, with parts dating to the 4th century BC. Its long evolution provides key evidence about how elite Roman homes developed over time.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:469a11d9-f643-4a16-a4d5-bd02ffa78850:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description "One of Pompeii''s oldest houses, with parts dating to the 4th century BC. Its long evolution provides key evidence about how elite Roman homes developed over time." restored on House of Sallust, Pompeii', '078b548aeab07d57875d231a62ed623767a0ac695a4effb5cf2e58314d581f37|901f4e398128833f627a34bfaca7c5b289c823f9039531d1f9a6df957d0d31e2|901f4e398128833f627a34bfaca7c5b289c823f9039531d1f9a6df957d0d31e2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "An election notice on its facade, backing Gaius Sallustius for office, named the House of Sallust, among the most sumptuous homes of Pompeii, its oldest parts dated to the 4th century BCE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "an election notice was placed on the facade [S5]; the notice recommended Gaius Sallustius for office [S5]; the house got its (modern) name from that notice [S5]; it was among the most sumptuous homes of Pompeii [S1]; its oldest parts are dated to the 4th century BCE [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-verify-025 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The House of Sallust is an elite residence (a house, domus) in Pompeii [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Sallust]; It is among the most sumptuous homes of Pompeii [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Sallust]; An election notice on its facade named the house [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Sallust]; The election notice backed (recommended) Gaius Sallustius for office [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Sallust]; The oldest parts of the house date to the 4th century BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Sallust]; The site is in Pompeii, Italy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompeii]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('473987c8-f684-4c4a-8c23-633821731f1e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Some 2,650 m of carefully cut stone once ringed Corduba: the Roman Walls of Córdoba, built after the Romans took the city in 206 BC. Vestiges remain in the Alcázar and near the Roman bridge.', 'Built in 206 BC after Rome seized the city, the walls ran 2,650 metres with a rubble-filled gap between inner and outer faces. Semicircular towers punctuated the circuit.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:473987c8-f684-4c4a-8c23-633821731f1e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Built in 206 BC after Rome seized the city, the walls ran 2,650 metres with a rubble-filled gap between inner and outer faces. Semicircular towers punctuated the circuit.'' restored on Roman Walls of Córdoba', '5767b547e227dff3c7038914af7e19835869015208f589d9742c9f8946d69bd3|9644638533ea80d4ccd4698af1996df4b0841d95ea87433a1e51a1bde4c698fa|9644638533ea80d4ccd4698af1996df4b0841d95ea87433a1e51a1bde4c698fa', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Some 2,650 m of carefully cut stone once ringed Corduba: the Roman Walls of Córdoba, built after the Romans took the city in 206 BC. Vestiges remain in the Alcázar and near the Roman bridge."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the walls stretched some 2,650 m [S3]; they were made of carefully cut stone [S4]; they once ringed (completely surrounded) the city [S3]; the city was Corduba [S1]; they were built after the Romans took the city [S2, S3]; the Romans took the city in 206 BC [S2]; vestiges remain in the Alcázar [S6]; vestiges remain near the Roman bridge [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Walls of Córdoba are the ancient Roman defensive walls of the Roman colonia of Corduba [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; Corduba is present-day Córdoba, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; The walls were about 2,650 m long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; The walls consisted of carefully cut stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; The walls once ringed the whole city of Corduba [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; The Romans took the city in 206 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; The walls were built after the Romans took the city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; Vestiges of the walls remain in the Alcázar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]; Vestiges of the walls remain near the Roman bridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_walls_of_C%C3%B3rdoba]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('49037cfb-5c88-4863-aa23-c317502fc6d8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Monastic cells ring a courtyard at Takht-i-Bahi, a Buddhist monastery of local stone near Mardan, considered among the most important relics of Buddhism in what was once Gandhara.', 'All of the buildings on the site are constructed from local stone and are mortared with lime and mud.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:49037cfb-5c88-4863-aa23-c317502fc6d8:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''All of the buildings on the site are constructed from local stone and are mortared with lime and mud.'' restored on Takht-i-Bahi', '31a5de2dab84dafc397c66ea15e0ae3dd5b66807c6c916306c1865376b257c53|f8eae67efc5d6395eb0818223dd6c033f3e19a0a8c15dde121076c85abbea28e|f8eae67efc5d6395eb0818223dd6c033f3e19a0a8c15dde121076c85abbea28e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monastic cells ring a courtyard at Takht-i-Bahi, a Buddhist monastery of local stone near Mardan, considered among the most important relics of Buddhism in what was once Gandhara."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "monastic cells are arranged around a courtyard [S5]; Takht-i-Bahi is a Buddhist monastery [S1]; it is built of local stone [S6]; it lies near Mardan [S1, S4]; it is considered among the most important relics of Buddhism in what was once Gandhara [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Takht-i-Bahi is an ancient Buddhist monastery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takht-i-Bahi]; Takht-i-Bahi lies near Mardan, Pakistan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takht-i-Bahi]; Monastic cells are arranged around a courtyard at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takht-i-Bahi]; The buildings are of local stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takht-i-Bahi]; The site is considered among the most important relics of Buddhism [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takht-i-Bahi]; The site lies in what was once Gandhara [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takht-i-Bahi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('498589cb-b378-4a09-adc3-3a911dc1cd77'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Two lines of basalt stones, said to resemble a giant eel, form the Lake Bolac Stone Arrangement, an Aboriginal ceremonial site in Victoria where some stones once gave way to a road.', 'Basalt stones in two lines shaped like a giant eel, an Aboriginal ceremonial site in Victoria. Stones at Lake Bolac range from 30 to 150 cm, some possibly embedded in the ground as anchors.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:498589cb-b378-4a09-adc3-3a911dc1cd77:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Basalt stones in two lines shaped like a giant eel, an Aboriginal ceremonial site in Victoria. Stones at Lake Bolac range from 30 to 150 cm, some possibly embedded in the ground as anchors.'' restored on Lake Bolac Stone Arrangement', '5269d7874857d8da6af74c806e8ac4b3c0bfdf189046ad744f3ca13d40ede161|d418849d5268beea598609c9ccb99dd196aa8f21c80809fdb9434c6dbb9e9ec1|d418849d5268beea598609c9ccb99dd196aa8f21c80809fdb9434c6dbb9e9ec1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Two lines of basalt stones, said to resemble a giant eel, form the Lake Bolac Stone Arrangement, an Aboriginal ceremonial site in Victoria where some stones once gave way to a road."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the stones are arranged in two lines [S4]; the stones are basalt [S4]; the arrangement is said to resemble a giant eel [S4]; it is an Aboriginal ceremonial site [S1]; it is in Victoria [S1]; some stones were removed to make way for a road [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Lake Bolac Stone Arrangement is an Aboriginal ceremonial site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Bolac_stone_arrangement]; The site is in Victoria, Australia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Bolac_stone_arrangement]; The arrangement is of basalt stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Bolac_stone_arrangement]; The stones form two lines [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Bolac_stone_arrangement]; The two lines of stones are said to resemble a giant eel [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Bolac_stone_arrangement]; Some of the stones were removed to make way for a road [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Bolac_stone_arrangement]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4a07cc38-1b55-4254-b0ad-fe875310caf7'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'A Roman temple to Augustus built 27 BC–14 AD with a richly decorated frieze. It survived by being repurposed as a church, then a granary, then a museum.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4a07cc38-1b55-4254-b0ad-fe875310caf7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A Roman temple to Augustus built 27 BC–14 AD with a richly decorated frieze. It survived by being repurposed as a church, then a granary, then a museum.'' restored on Temple of Augustus, Split', '||82a4bedb7bac8bc0fa07f4287fefaea2839b0754c045e038ae5b32e4e384b462', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: failed-after-two-rewrites", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The card is not about this site.; The site is named ''Temple of Augustus, Split'', but S1 places the Temple of Augustus that the whole description (and so the card) describes in Pula, so the card may be about a namesake in Pula rather than this site; the site''s name or its description must be reconciled before a card is written.; The card is not about this site.; The site is Temple of Augustus, Split, but the fact basis (S1) places the Temple of Augustus in the city of Pula; every fact on the card (bronze dedication letters, Corinthian porch, Byzantine church conversion) comes from sentences about the Pula temple, a namesake, so the card is not shown to be about the Split site and the basis must be corrected or the card withdrawn.; The card is not about this site.; The site is named ''Temple of Augustus, Split'', but the card places the temple in Pula (as S1 does); no sentence ties the temple to Split, so the card describes the Pula temple, a namesake, and not this site. The site/description mismatch must be resolved before this card can be used."}]'::jsonb),
    ('4a4e2c30-8a8f-4b7d-8e7c-c0e837b94760'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a plateau 3,625 metres up, the Inca centre of Huánuco Pampa opens onto an immense plaza 550m by 350m, with 497 storehouses and the Qhapaq Ñan passing through from Cusco to Quito.', 'At Huánuco Pampa the immense rectangular plaza measured 550m by 350m which is more than 19 hectares.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4a4e2c30-8a8f-4b7d-8e7c-c0e837b94760:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''At Huánuco Pampa the immense rectangular plaza measured 550m by 350m which is more than 19 hectares.'' restored on Huánuco Pampa', '20a0f0f255cdba54ee6d593224a72839b95deaa50ca9a2e9ea53fbfc12f127ce|b338b8de784e32e774c59250f775834f02f22646567f27aca4cb89f2fc9b2078|b338b8de784e32e774c59250f775834f02f22646567f27aca4cb89f2fc9b2078', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a plateau 3,625 metres up, the Inca centre of Huánuco Pampa opens onto an immense plaza 550m by 350m, with 497 storehouses and the Qhapaq Ñan passing through from Cusco to Quito."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on a plateau [S3]; it lies at an elevation of 3,625 metres [S3]; it was an Inca (administrative) centre [S2]; it has an immense plaza [S4]; the plaza measures 550m by 350m [S4]; it has 497 storehouses [S7]; the Qhapaq Ñan passes through it [S5]; the Qhapaq Ñan runs from Cusco to Quito [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huánuco Pampa is an Inca administrative centre [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; Huánuco Pampa is in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; It stands on a plateau [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; The plateau is at an elevation of 3,625 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; It has an immense plaza [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; The plaza measured 550 m by 350 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; The site has 497 storehouses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; The Qhapaq Ñan passed through Huánuco Pampa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]; The Qhapaq Ñan ran from Cusco to Quito [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hu%C3%A1nuco_Pampa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4bde4866-94ec-41c2-a7c5-d38e3a2280ea'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In a limestone cliff above the river Samson, the Goyet Caves yielded 99 Neanderthal bones from at least five individuals, and their condition strongly suggests cannibalism.', 'Caves with 120,000 years of continuous occupation — from Neanderthals to early modern humans. Thousands of fossils document the full transition between the two species.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4bde4866-94ec-41c2-a7c5-d38e3a2280ea:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Caves with 120,000 years of continuous occupation — from Neanderthals to early modern humans. Thousands of fossils document the full transition between the two species.'' restored on Goyet Caves', 'a8685f60b1fb3242f38ded0f42b36634b854d0fc88b2e3ff6a0cb5c85d05dc59|564699878a0cfd73bd09886382b5d455c6fa64a5f34afc5640c754da63a2c843|564699878a0cfd73bd09886382b5d455c6fa64a5f34afc5640c754da63a2c843', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In a limestone cliff above the river Samson, the Goyet Caves yielded 99 Neanderthal bones from at least five individuals, and their condition strongly suggests cannibalism."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the caves are in a limestone cliff [S1]; the cliff is above the river Samson [S1]; the caves yielded 99 Neanderthal bones [S3]; the bones belong to at least five individuals [S3]; the condition of the bones strongly suggests cannibalism [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Goyet Caves are a series of connected caves in Belgium in a limestone cliff [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goyet_Caves]; The cliff lies above the river Samson [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goyet_Caves]; The caves yielded 99 Neanderthal bones [SUPPORTED: https://www.nature.com/articles/srep29005]; The Neanderthal bones come from at least five individuals [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goyet_Caves]; The condition of the bones strongly suggests cannibalism [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goyet_Caves]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4c0798e4-c439-490e-a8b7-60a0bad11cfd'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Maumbury Rings, a Neolithic henge in Dorchester, hides a ditch of up to 45 shafts cut up to 11 metres deep into the chalk. In the Roman period it was remodelled as an amphitheatre.', 'Maumbury Rings was remodelled in the Roman period when it was adapted for use as an amphitheatre for the use of the citizens of the nearby Roman town of Durnovaria.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4c0798e4-c439-490e-a8b7-60a0bad11cfd:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Maumbury Rings was remodelled in the Roman period when it was adapted for use as an amphitheatre for the use of the citizens of the nearby Roman town of Durnovaria.'' restored on Maumbury Rings', '7725ae3020e271bd5e6c2c78b5711108db3597dc1227c14502381ae0b12adba4|4a499d323b9e822037e618abda2836ee480f2f1cf88a9cdaa5c546ae9b51be13|4a499d323b9e822037e618abda2836ee480f2f1cf88a9cdaa5c546ae9b51be13', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Maumbury Rings, a Neolithic henge in Dorchester, hides a ditch of up to 45 shafts cut up to 11 metres deep into the chalk. In the Roman period it was remodelled as an amphitheatre."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Maumbury Rings is a Neolithic henge [S1]; it is in Dorchester [S1]; it has a ditch made of a series of shafts [S5]; the shafts number up to 45 [S6]; the shafts are up to 11 metres deep [S6]; the shafts are cut into the chalk [S5]; it was remodelled as an amphitheatre in the Roman period [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Maumbury Rings is a henge in Dorchester [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; The henge is Neolithic in date [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; It had a ditch made up of shafts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; The shafts were cut into the chalk [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; The ditch has up to 45 shafts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; The shafts are up to 11 metres deep [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; The shaft ditch is hidden (no longer visible on the surface) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; The site was remodelled in the Roman period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]; The Roman remodelling made it an amphitheatre [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maumbury_Rings]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4c40a3eb-e009-4b8b-b507-18053a6538a1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'River silt has left the Heraion at Foce del Sele, a Greek sanctuary of Hera once at the mouth of the Sele, about 2.3 km from the coast. Some seventy sandstone metopes were found there.', 'The ancient sanctuary was founded at the beginning of the sixth century BC by the Greek colony at Paestum who originally were from further south at Sybaris.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4c40a3eb-e009-4b8b-b507-18053a6538a1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The ancient sanctuary was founded at the beginning of the sixth century BC by the Greek colony at Paestum who originally were from further south at Sybaris.'' restored on Heraion at Foce del Sele', '38ab5c7590cc9ba405d6bd1b103a8c881f7e4482633d07f0754d6c7fce02dd23|8e3b21922753c7ed5a4d479a951d9682f00ae06faf1fd2845298805b32614030|8e3b21922753c7ed5a4d479a951d9682f00ae06faf1fd2845298805b32614030', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "River silt has left the Heraion at Foce del Sele, a Greek sanctuary of Hera once at the mouth of the Sele, about 2.3 km from the coast. Some seventy sandstone metopes were found there."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "river sediment has left the site about 2.3 km from the coast [S3]; it is a Greek sanctuary [S1]; the sanctuary is dedicated to Hera [S1]; it was once at the mouth of the Sele [S2]; some seventy metopes were found there [S6]; the metopes are of sandstone [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Heraion at Foce del Sele is a sanctuary dedicated to the goddess Hera [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraion_at_Foce_del_Sele]; It is a Greek sanctuary [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraion_at_Foce_del_Sele]; It was once located at the mouth of the Sele [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraion_at_Foce_del_Sele]; River silt (alluvial sediment) has moved the coast away from the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraion_at_Foce_del_Sele]; The site is now about 2.3 km from the coast [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraion_at_Foce_del_Sele]; About seventy metopes were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraion_at_Foce_del_Sele]; The metopes are of sandstone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraion_at_Foce_del_Sele]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4c650823-6f85-4e10-9976-61cf1369a9b9'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Anglesey, Presaddfed Burial Chamber holds two Neolithic tombs: one still bearing a capstone some 4 by 3 m, the other with its fallen capstone leaning against two remaining uprights.', 'The southern tomb is the more complete; it has a capstone some 4 by 3 m, supported by three stout upright stones at the south end and by a single more slender stone at the north.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4c650823-6f85-4e10-9976-61cf1369a9b9:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The southern tomb is the more complete; it has a capstone some 4 by 3 m, supported by three stout upright stones at the south end and by a single more slender stone at the north.'' restored on Presaddfed Burial Chamber', '9c157fde9fecaac8452ec68bf3f0b9633dc3ed8ae6a606b0d2024d51b524bc05|ff8fc94802a7c15f80ee2fcde1d9016acff335f74a7286714282ab76b26ea237|ff8fc94802a7c15f80ee2fcde1d9016acff335f74a7286714282ab76b26ea237', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Anglesey, Presaddfed Burial Chamber holds two Neolithic tombs: one still bearing a capstone some 4 by 3 m, the other with its fallen capstone leaning against two remaining uprights."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on Anglesey [S1]; it comprises two tombs [S1]; the tombs are Neolithic [S1]; one tomb still bears a capstone [S2]; that capstone is some 4 by 3 m [S2]; the other tomb''s capstone has fallen [S3]; the fallen capstone leans against two remaining uprights [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Presaddfed Burial Chamber is a burial site (chamber tombs) on Anglesey [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presaddfed_Burial_Chamber]; The site holds two tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presaddfed_Burial_Chamber]; The tombs are Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presaddfed_Burial_Chamber]; One tomb still bears a capstone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presaddfed_Burial_Chamber]; That capstone measures some 4 by 3 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presaddfed_Burial_Chamber]; The other tomb has two remaining uprights [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presaddfed_Burial_Chamber]; The other tomb''s capstone has fallen and leans against the uprights [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presaddfed_Burial_Chamber]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4c6ce4c1-8c8c-4982-a0fc-8df781a35ef6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A village sits inside Avebury, a Neolithic henge 347.4 metres in diameter that holds three stone circles, while an avenue of paired stones leads out from its southeastern entrance.', 'By the Iron Age, the site had been effectively abandoned, with some evidence of human activity on the site during the Roman period.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4c6ce4c1-8c8c-4982-a0fc-8df781a35ef6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''By the Iron Age, the site had been effectively abandoned, with some evidence of human activity on the site during the Roman period.'' restored on Avebury', 'dda13b85d9173eee6d3af6c54d2dbbe85b45da890e7fdcee29e2b8f1ae595df9|f53b73d9dd92af5c6d921b0df3781bb152614d5344735e65fd5496261c820833|f53b73d9dd92af5c6d921b0df3781bb152614d5344735e65fd5496261c820833', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A village sits inside Avebury, a Neolithic henge 347.4 metres in diameter that holds three stone circles, while an avenue of paired stones leads out from its southeastern entrance."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a village sits inside the monument (the henge lies around the village) [S1]; Avebury is a Neolithic henge [S1]; the henge measures 347.4 metres in diameter [S5]; it contains three stone circles [S1]; an avenue of paired stones leads out from its southeastern entrance [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Avebury is a Neolithic henge monument (in Wiltshire, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avebury]; A village sits inside Avebury [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avebury]; The henge is 347.4 metres in diameter [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avebury]; The henge holds three stone circles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avebury]; An avenue of paired stones leads out from its southeastern entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avebury]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4d5292ae-6594-45be-a2cd-e2f7cac4c108'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Carved into the limestone of Mount Harşena, the Pontic Rock Tombs of Amasya rise 8 to 15 m high: the royal necropolis of the Pontic kings, with big stone grave chambers inside.', 'Monumental rock-carved tombs of the Pontic kings, 8 to 15 metres high, cut into a 272-metre-high limestone cliff. The royal tomb chambers are hewn deep into the stone.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4d5292ae-6594-45be-a2cd-e2f7cac4c108:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Monumental rock-carved tombs of the Pontic kings, 8 to 15 metres high, cut into a 272-metre-high limestone cliff. The royal tomb chambers are hewn deep into the stone.'' restored on Pontic Rock Tombs of Amasya', 'c0a81f431c2968a3c8e3aafd4002ea04ca6d5f5bb26d6204dc8e80b4d7836fd0|f6512d3dd1dcd33e79d1f4effb82499020970646a3c4bba63c68ab36089b08c5|f6512d3dd1dcd33e79d1f4effb82499020970646a3c4bba63c68ab36089b08c5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Carved into the limestone of Mount Harşena, the Pontic Rock Tombs of Amasya rise 8 to 15 m high: the royal necropolis of the Pontic kings, with big stone grave chambers inside."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the tombs are carved into limestone rock [S3]; they are on Mount Harşena [S2]; the rock tombs are 8 to 15 m high [S7]; they form the royal necropolis of the Pontic kings [S1]; the tombs contain big stone grave chambers inside [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Pontic Rock Tombs of Amasya are rock-carved tombs forming the royal necropolis of the Pontic kings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tombs_of_the_kings_of_Pontus]; The tombs are on Mount Harşena [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tombs_of_the_kings_of_Pontus]; The tombs are carved into limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tombs_of_the_kings_of_Pontus]; The tombs rise 8 to 15 m high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tombs_of_the_kings_of_Pontus]; The tombs contain big stone grave chambers inside [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tombs_of_the_kings_of_Pontus]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4d9739d5-0517-4d26-a66c-d668e20f745a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Maya farming village of Cuello holds the remains of a steam bath from approximately 900 BC, the oldest found to date in the Maya lowlands, and a mass burial of sacrificed men.', '1200 BC Maya farming village with a long occupational history. Inhabitants built on plaster-coated platforms around central patios. A steam bath dated ~900 BC is the oldest known in the Maya lowlands.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4d9739d5-0517-4d26-a66c-d668e20f745a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''1200 BC Maya farming village with a long occupational history. Inhabitants built on plaster-coated platforms around central patios. A steam bath dated ~900 BC is the oldest known in the Maya lowlands.'' restored on Cuello', '5582779da7f4da3d99fafa9fb5ec8582e1563ea2b3e93060844db136491d62aa|4896125c90050b1bb17ebbe5eb4b4b95ee92544047e404f5a9de31e71dea78f9|4896125c90050b1bb17ebbe5eb4b4b95ee92544047e404f5a9de31e71dea78f9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Maya farming village of Cuello holds the remains of a steam bath from approximately 900 BC, the oldest found to date in the Maya lowlands, and a mass burial of sacrificed men."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cuello is a Maya site [S1]; Cuello was a farming village [S2]; it holds the remains of a steam bath [S6]; the steam bath dates to approximately 900 BC [S6]; it is the oldest steam bath found to date in the Maya lowlands [S6]; it holds a mass burial of men who had been sacrificed [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-028 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cuello is a Maya archaeological site in Belize [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuello]; Cuello is a farming village (a settlement) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuello]; Cuello holds the remains of a steam bath [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuello]; The steam bath dates to approximately 900 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuello]; The steam bath is the oldest found to date in the Maya lowlands [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuello]; Cuello has a mass burial [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuello]; The mass burial contained sacrificed men (males) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuello]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4dc60f9f-66cf-4a4a-9c47-bf24cc0d618d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Carved from a single piece of stone, the Gate of the Sun was found lying horizontally with a large crack through it. Its carvings may have served a calendrical purpose.', 'The Gate of the Sun is approximately 9.8 ft tall and 13 ft wide, and was carved from a single piece of stone.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:4dc60f9f-66cf-4a4a-9c47-bf24cc0d618d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The Gate of the Sun is approximately 9.8 ft tall and 13 ft wide, and was carved from a single piece of stone.'' restored on Gate of the Sun', '3044877e039b8f48a4d85e2e449305837ac6106ba3d788b92b17185a42ecf942|2cb6b6eceae53d091d5d0340373023af4a40ca2621ce2190a77ceb351f6c43d8|2cb6b6eceae53d091d5d0340373023af4a40ca2621ce2190a77ceb351f6c43d8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Carved from a single piece of stone, the Gate of the Sun was found lying horizontally with a large crack through it. Its carvings may have served a calendrical purpose."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Gate of the Sun was carved from a single piece of stone [S3]; it was found lying horizontally [S5]; it had a large crack through it when found [S5]; its carvings may have served a calendrical purpose (hedged) [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-028 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Gate of the Sun is a monolithic gateway at Tiwanaku (Tiahuanaco), Bolivia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gate_of_the_Sun]; The Gate of the Sun was carved from a single piece of stone [SUPPORTED