    ('51ec5b10-cd97-4fc2-a827-fc265910d873'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a hill near Saelices lies Segobriga, a Celtic and Roman city whose Celtiberian name means something like Triumphant Fortress. In 1888, a Bronze Age burial ground was found in a cave.', 'A Celtic and Roman city on a hilltop, active from the 2nd century BC. It sat atop massive lapis specularis mines — "window glass" for the Roman world.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:51ec5b10-cd97-4fc2-a827-fc265910d873:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A Celtic and Roman city on a hilltop, active from the 2nd century BC. It sat atop massive lapis specularis mines — "window glass" for the Roman world.'' restored on Segobriga', '6fed07dc2def2b29ad204261196c9a13acb49a367b115362f0b96aa3a97fa854|9888b3725ff7c6e1c3702668741c2cd293f24079ba8778560a52eba7ae7f362e|9888b3725ff7c6e1c3702668741c2cd293f24079ba8778560a52eba7ae7f362e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a hill near Saelices lies Segobriga, a Celtic and Roman city whose Celtiberian name means something like Triumphant Fortress. In 1888, a Bronze Age burial ground was found in a cave."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Segobriga lies on a hill near Saelices [S1]; it was a Celtic and Roman city [S1]; its name is Celtiberian [S3]; the name means something like Triumphant Fortress (hedged) [S4]; in 1888 a Bronze Age burial ground was found in a cave [S5]; the cave belongs to Segobriga (cueva de Segóbriga) [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Segobriga is a Celtic and Roman city on a hill near Saelices (central claim: what the place is and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segobriga]; The name Segobriga is Celtiberian [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segobriga]; The name means something like Triumphant Fortress [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segobriga]; In 1888 a burial ground was found (date of the find) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segobriga]; The burial ground found in 1888 dates to the Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segobriga]; The burial ground was found in a cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segobriga]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('53325d71-8fe8-404a-8995-f4b2dd9d7d13'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Found on its side in 1969 and raised again around 1970, the Menhir of Outeiro is a granite monolith 5.6 meters high, weighing an estimated 8 tonnes, the second largest in Portugal.', 'A megalith known locally as Penedo Comprido — ''long boulder'' — found on its side in 1969 and later re-erected. It stands near the border, part of a megalithic landscape spanning two countries.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:53325d71-8fe8-404a-8995-f4b2dd9d7d13:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description "A megalith known locally as Penedo Comprido — ''long boulder'' — found on its side in 1969 and later re-erected. It stands near the border, part of a megalithic landscape spanning two countries." restored on Menhir of Outeiro', '831661c4a01629b4eefa906249e5b6e694e065f3d9e4ada7f5577859df31dda0|1657d1dc112d89eb68cb3a118f77220cea02a198ef1da94bc185dfadfb240e50|1657d1dc112d89eb68cb3a118f77220cea02a198ef1da94bc185dfadfb240e50', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Found on its side in 1969 and raised again around 1970, the Menhir of Outeiro is a granite monolith 5.6 meters high, weighing an estimated 8 tonnes, the second largest in Portugal."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the menhir was found on its side in 1969 [S2]; it was raised again around 1970 [S2]; it is a granite monolith [S4]; it is 5.6 meters high [S4]; it weighs an estimated 8 tonnes [S4]; it is the second largest in Portugal [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Menhir of Outeiro is a menhir (megalith) in Portugal (central claim: what the site is and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Outeiro]; The stone was found lying on its side in 1969 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Outeiro]; It was raised again around 1970 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Outeiro]; It is a granite monolith [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Outeiro]; It is 5.6 meters high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Outeiro]; It weighs an estimated 8 tonnes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Outeiro]; It is the second largest menhir in Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhir_of_Outeiro]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('533ef43f-8c4a-47bf-bdeb-d6662ae50e68'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Levänluhta, a spring in Isokyrö, was an Iron Age water graveyard from the 5th to the 8th centuries, the waterlogged ground keeping the remains of around 100 individuals exceptionally well.', 'An Iron Age water burial site where about 100 individuals were placed in a spring over three centuries. The waterlogged environment preserved bones that normally dissolve in acidic Nordic soils.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:533ef43f-8c4a-47bf-bdeb-d6662ae50e68:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''An Iron Age water burial site where about 100 individuals were placed in a spring over three centuries. The waterlogged environment preserved bones that normally dissolve in acidic Nordic soils.'' restored on Levänluhta', '0b5726437bfc18fdab4bd9227e94b98df65a901549858c6bba307a7065d4c317|aa07b637fe2fad0dce4f919296c6f90658ab5b06c8b795c65aa2889779e690db|aa07b637fe2fad0dce4f919296c6f90658ab5b06c8b795c65aa2889779e690db', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Levänluhta, a spring in Isokyrö, was an Iron Age water graveyard from the 5th to the 8th centuries, the waterlogged ground keeping the remains of around 100 individuals exceptionally well."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Levänluhta is a spring [S1]; it is in Isokyrö [S1]; it was an Iron Age water graveyard [S2]; it was used from the 5th to the 8th centuries [S2]; it contains the remains of around 100 individuals [S2]; the waterlogged environment preserved the remains exceptionally well [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Levänluhta is a spring (and archaeological site) (central claim: what the site is) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev%C3%A4nluhta]; The spring lies in Isokyrö (Finland) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev%C3%A4nluhta]; It was used as a water graveyard [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev%C3%A4nluhta]; The water graveyard dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev%C3%A4nluhta]; It was used from the 5th to the 8th centuries [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev%C3%A4nluhta]; It contains the remains of around 100 individuals [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev%C3%A4nluhta]; The waterlogged ground kept the remains exceptionally well [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev%C3%A4nluhta]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('54c10788-8f83-470d-9a0b-6eb03f502572'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Named after the Earth Maker, the creator god Pacha Kamaq, Pachacamac in the Valley of the Lurín River flourished for about 1,300 years until the Spanish invaded.', 'Named after the creator god Pacha Kamaq, this oracle site drew pilgrims for 1,300 years from 200 to 1500 AD. Even the Inca incorporated this powerful deity into their pantheon.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:54c10788-8f83-470d-9a0b-6eb03f502572:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Named after the creator god Pacha Kamaq, this oracle site drew pilgrims for 1,300 years from 200 to 1500 AD. Even the Inca incorporated this powerful deity into their pantheon.'' restored on Pachacamac', '114b68e7d7d7721982a474e623baabb1a4cdd2f986bc98167fdeee4e3f8109b9|9be119e021f99003cb750500329059c79cc9feedf545aa82db26b7537a1102e0|9be119e021f99003cb750500329059c79cc9feedf545aa82db26b7537a1102e0', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Named after the Earth Maker, the creator god Pacha Kamaq, Pachacamac in the Valley of the Lurín River flourished for about 1,300 years until the Spanish invaded."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pachacamac is named after the creator god Pacha Kamaq, the Earth Maker [S2]; it is in the Valley of the Lurín River [S1]; it flourished for about 1,300 years [S3]; it flourished until the Spanish invaded [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pachacamac is an archaeological site in the Valley of the Lurín River, Peru (central claim: what the site is and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachacamac]; The site is named after the Earth Maker [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachacamac]; The Earth Maker is the creator god Pacha Kamaq [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachacamac]; The site flourished for about 1,300 years [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachacamac]; Its flourishing ended with the Spanish invasion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachacamac]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('54e2461b-5a65-4da6-9e73-10b14a813736'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the summit of a mountain that shares its name, about 4,000 m up in the Cusco Region, lies Mullu Q''awa, an archaeological site declared a National Cultural Heritage in 2010.', 'The site was declared a National Cultural Heritage by Resolution No. 954-2010- INC on April 27, 2010.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:54e2461b-5a65-4da6-9e73-10b14a813736:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The site was declared a National Cultural Heritage by Resolution No. 954-2010- INC on April 27, 2010.'' restored on Mullu Q''awa', '73d7f1414edbe8a1f4f7bb69de5c57c3dfae9bedfae86c81c90fd9c2e927b5c8|40f814c78571181d83cdd64cdf3aa596e86c10457180c92a91d64383e461f739|40f814c78571181d83cdd64cdf3aa596e86c10457180c92a91d64383e461f739', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the summit of a mountain that shares its name, about 4,000 m up in the Cusco Region, lies Mullu Q''awa, an archaeological site declared a National Cultural Heritage in 2010."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Mullu Q''awa is an archaeological site [S1]; it lies on a mountain that shares its name [S1]; it lies on the summit of that mountain [S4]; it is at a height of about 4,000 m [S4]; it is in the Cusco Region [S2]; it was declared a National Cultural Heritage [S5]; the declaration was made in 2010 [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Mullu Q''awa is an archaeological site (central claim: what the site is) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullu_Q%27awa]; The site lies on a mountain that shares its name [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullu_Q%27awa]; It is in the Cusco Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullu_Q%27awa]; It is situated on the summit of the mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullu_Q%27awa]; It lies about 4,000 m up [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullu_Q%27awa]; It was declared a National Cultural Heritage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullu_Q%27awa]; The declaration was made in 2010 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullu_Q%27awa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('54e625b7-a84e-4502-b43a-e65734d928c7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Found in 1998 in Larnaca, the Lefkaritis Tomb was an un-looted chamber sealed nearly 2800 years ago in the city-kingdom of Kition. Among its few objects: extraordinary gold ornaments.', '8th-century BC tomb sealed for nearly 2,800 years, found in 1998 inside the walls of city-kingdom Kition. The unlooted Lefkaritis hypogeum held extraordinary gold ornaments now conserved in a museum.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:54e625b7-a84e-4502-b43a-e65734d928c7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''8th-century BC tomb sealed for nearly 2,800 years, found in 1998 inside the walls of city-kingdom Kition. The unlooted Lefkaritis hypogeum held extraordinary gold ornaments now conserved in a museum.'' restored on Lefkaritis Tomb', 'fa6dcabdf339548a869dfcb941f19c2977fa971e84bd2447fb30ae90d1a7732c|3a2363879c314a0a78b5802b2a8af5f5b99b1b2da44950b4319679e598f3b72d|3a2363879c314a0a78b5802b2a8af5f5b99b1b2da44950b4319679e598f3b72d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Found in 1998 in Larnaca, the Lefkaritis Tomb was an un-looted chamber sealed nearly 2800 years ago in the city-kingdom of Kition. Among its few objects: extraordinary gold ornaments."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Lefkaritis Tomb was found in 1998 [S2]; it is in Larnaca [S1]; it was an un-looted chamber [S2]; the chamber had been sealed nearly 2800 years ago [S2]; it lies in the city-kingdom of Kition [S2]; few objects were found in it [S3]; among the objects were extraordinary gold ornaments [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-031 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Lefkaritis Tomb is a tomb (hypogeum) in Larnaca, Cyprus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]; It was found/discovered in 1998 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]; It was an un-looted chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]; The chamber had been sealed nearly 2800 years ago [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]; The tomb was found in Larnaca [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]; The tomb lay in the city-kingdom of Kition [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]; Only a few objects were found in it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]; Among the objects were extraordinary gold ornaments [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lefkaritis_Tomb]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('55ac13ab-3693-4e65-af02-a6b424d0e345'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Built outside the town walls, the Roman Τheatre of Bosra was later completely enclosed by an Ayyubid fortress. It is 102 metres across, with seating for about 17,000 people.', 'A 2nd-century AD theatre built from black basalt. Its dark stone gives it a dramatically different look from any other Roman theatre.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:55ac13ab-3693-4e65-af02-a6b424d0e345:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A 2nd-century AD theatre built from black basalt. Its dark stone gives it a dramatically different look from any other Roman theatre.'' restored on Roman Τheatre of Bosra', '5d887cdbfbe6dd27ae0eebedaae610c81ccd5e9238f780a3ab24101306beed99|fd20d2ece8bc626d613ada3c4d6a4e0dca02bf95eef44f33ba002938bf699f07|fd20d2ece8bc626d613ada3c4d6a4e0dca02bf95eef44f33ba002938bf699f07', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built outside the town walls, the Roman Τheatre of Bosra was later completely enclosed by an Ayyubid fortress. It is 102 metres across, with seating for about 17,000 people."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Roman Theatre of Bosra was built outside the town walls [S2]; it was later completely enclosed by an Ayyubid fortress [S2]; it is 102 metres across [S3]; it has seating for about 17,000 people [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-031 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Theatre of Bosra is a Roman theatre in Bosra, Syria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_at_Bosra]; It was built outside the town walls and later completely enclosed by an Ayyubid fortress [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_at_Bosra]; It is 102 metres across [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_at_Bosra]; It has seating for about 17,000 people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_at_Bosra]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('55f1b595-fef2-4d16-9dfc-fb5e22634b07'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Ilkley, the stones of the Twelve Apostles are made of local millstone grit, and by the mid-20th-century every one of them had fallen and lay loose upon the ground.', 'A stone circle of 12 remaining millstone grit blocks, originally 16-20 stones, spanning 15 meters in diameter. All stones had toppled by the mid-20th century before restoration.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:55f1b595-fef2-4d16-9dfc-fb5e22634b07:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A stone circle of 12 remaining millstone grit blocks, originally 16-20 stones, spanning 15 meters in diameter. All stones had toppled by the mid-20th century before restoration.'' restored on Twelve Apostles, West Yorkshire', '9b1ff0df334809eb42941dc46877982bf04d27c8069cbbfc11418c0883f2b7c5|b84c7aeb5f4c9144e9f113978106c11d795b3c697ed18039b865075540a5d6f3|b84c7aeb5f4c9144e9f113978106c11d795b3c697ed18039b865075540a5d6f3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Ilkley, the stones of the Twelve Apostles are made of local millstone grit, and by the mid-20th-century every one of them had fallen and lay loose upon the ground."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Twelve Apostles are near Ilkley [S1]; the stones are made of local millstone grit [S5]; by the mid-20th-century every stone had fallen [S6]; the fallen stones lay loose upon the ground [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-002 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Twelve Apostles is a stone circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Apostles,_West_Yorkshire]; It is near Ilkley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Apostles,_West_Yorkshire]; The stones are made of local millstone grit [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Apostles,_West_Yorkshire]; By the mid-20th century every stone had fallen and lay loose on the ground [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Apostles,_West_Yorkshire]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('565a3dc3-f8a7-4d38-a672-17c57f9401e9'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Pyongyang, a crescent-shaped wall about 3450 metres long was built of alternating layers of soil, rock and sand: the Chongam-ri Earthen Castle, thought to date to the Gojoseon period.', '2nd-century BC Gojoseon fortification near Pyongyang, crescent-shaped walls 3,450 m long, built in alternating soil, rock, and sand layers. A gilded crown dissolved in mercury was found here.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:565a3dc3-f8a7-4d38-a672-17c57f9401e9:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''2nd-century BC Gojoseon fortification near Pyongyang, crescent-shaped walls 3,450 m long, built in alternating soil, rock, and sand layers. A gilded crown dissolved in mercury was found here.'' restored on Chongam-ri Earthen Castle', '30bd3df72f05d7b4e4492469637fcf919e7d5f733816f40d35c86c3d2ea4b5c8|15b89950c0c3b39a25e5cef793fc842468513925b2e6bedd7fab01fc8e7c18b8|15b89950c0c3b39a25e5cef793fc842468513925b2e6bedd7fab01fc8e7c18b8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Pyongyang, a crescent-shaped wall about 3450 metres long was built of alternating layers of soil, rock and sand: the Chongam-ri Earthen Castle, thought to date to the Gojoseon period."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Chongam-ri Earthen Castle is near Pyongyang [S2]; its wall is crescent-shaped [S5]; the wall is about 3450 metres long [S5]; it was built of alternating layers of soil, rock and sand [S3]; it is thought to date to the Gojoseon period [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-031 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chongam-ri Earthen Castle is an earthen fortification (castle) near Pyongyang, North Korea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chongam-ri_Earthen_Castle]; It is thought to date to the Gojoseon period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chongam-ri_Earthen_Castle]; It was built of alternating layers of soil, rock and sand [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chongam-ri_Earthen_Castle]; It is a wall / fortification [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chongam-ri_Earthen_Castle]; The wall is crescent-shaped [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chongam-ri_Earthen_Castle]; The wall is about 3450 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chongam-ri_Earthen_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('571c2899-adaa-4520-b758-3bdcaaf8d503'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Makran coast near the Iranian border lies Sutkagan Dor, the westernmost known site of the Indus Valley civilisation, where copper arrowheads and shell beads were found.', '2600–1900 BC westernmost known outpost of the Indus Valley civilisation, 480 km west of Karachi on the Makran coast. Sutkagan Dor''s stone walls protected a 4.5-hectare frontier settlement near Iran.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:571c2899-adaa-4520-b758-3bdcaaf8d503:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description "2600–1900 BC westernmost known outpost of the Indus Valley civilisation, 480 km west of Karachi on the Makran coast. Sutkagan Dor''s stone walls protected a 4.5-hectare frontier settlement near Iran." restored on Sutkagan Dor', '5230bee7c95a54c4a611b47ce1df87db8eaac0b1a392084ce7cbdf54fb1e094a|3c7803ae9b5ac6a43e7c7d492380485813fb0caaf23e5a06433ffb7f614a54ff|3c7803ae9b5ac6a43e7c7d492380485813fb0caaf23e5a06433ffb7f614a54ff', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Makran coast near the Iranian border lies Sutkagan Dor, the westernmost known site of the Indus Valley civilisation, where copper arrowheads and shell beads were found."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sutkagan Dor is on the Makran coast [S2]; it is near the Iranian border [S2]; it is the westernmost known site of the Indus Valley civilisation [S1]; copper arrowheads were found there [S8]; shell beads were found there [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-031 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sutkagan Dor is an archaeological site (a settlement with fortification) in Pakistan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutkagan_Dor]; It lies on the Makran coast near the Iranian border [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutkagan_Dor]; It is the westernmost known site of the Indus Valley civilisation [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutkagan_Dor]; Copper arrowheads were found there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutkagan_Dor]; Shell beads were found there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutkagan_Dor]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('574b24e0-9357-4d1a-a66b-556a0dfe6303'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Overlooking Yatton, Cadbury Hill is crowned by an Iron Age hill fort where remains of round houses may still be seen. Around 400, after the withdrawal of Rome, it was refortified.', 'The hill fort was refortified around 400, after the urban collapse associated with the withdrawal of Rome, and occupation extended into the sub-Roman period.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:574b24e0-9357-4d1a-a66b-556a0dfe6303:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The hill fort was refortified around 400, after the urban collapse associated with the withdrawal of Rome, and occupation extended into the sub-Roman period.'' restored on Cadbury Hill', '426b1cbcc01e313aa79374f53f41569734790c8143bd5153cbfa31809ba82152|2a4f00c4c7e5b8615609548ea2c0ec009f5b3c2b407454c205e513cb10b0c1c8|2a4f00c4c7e5b8615609548ea2c0ec009f5b3c2b407454c205e513cb10b0c1c8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Overlooking Yatton, Cadbury Hill is crowned by an Iron Age hill fort where remains of round houses may still be seen. Around 400, after the withdrawal of Rome, it was refortified."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cadbury Hill overlooks Yatton [S1]; an Iron Age hill fort stands on its summit [S2]; remains of round houses may still be seen inside the fort [S5]; it was refortified around 400 [S6]; the refortification came after the withdrawal of Rome [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cadbury Hill is a hill fort site (central claim: a hill with a hill fort on its summit) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Hill]; Cadbury Hill overlooks Yatton [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Hill]; The hill fort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Hill]; Remains of round houses may still be seen at the hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Hill]; The hill fort was refortified around the year 400 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Hill]; The refortification came after the withdrawal of Rome [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Hill]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('58b9666f-7ff2-4be0-9843-7f1c2b43592c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the Welsh Marches, the ramparts of Old Oswestry grew in four distinct phases around a large early Iron Age hill fort, occupied from the 8th century BC until the Roman conquest of Britain.', 'In 1939–40, geologist and amateur archaeologist William J. Varley conducted the most extensive examination of the site.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:58b9666f-7ff2-4be0-9843-7f1c2b43592c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''In 1939–40, geologist and amateur archaeologist William J. Varley conducted the most extensive examination of the site.'' restored on Old Oswestry', '5901b2df63a683ab31247bb86421cb6e892da064b387a23cfc3926cc716c8d3c|25bf65c989c14bf197d6705ef81a810f6868bfc34c6588f61d8327089cf36bd6|25bf65c989c14bf197d6705ef81a810f6868bfc34c6588f61d8327089cf36bd6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Welsh Marches, the ramparts of Old Oswestry grew in four distinct phases around a large early Iron Age hill fort, occupied from the 8th century BC until the Roman conquest of Britain."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Old Oswestry is in the Welsh Marches [S1]; its ramparts surround the hill fort [S6]; the ramparts developed in four distinct phases [S6]; it is a large early Iron Age hill fort [S1]; it was occupied from the 8th century BC [S3]; occupation continued until the Roman conquest of Britain [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Old Oswestry is a hill fort in the Welsh Marches (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Oswestry]; The hill fort is large [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Oswestry]; The hill fort is early Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Oswestry]; The ramparts grew in four distinct phases [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Oswestry]; The site was occupied from the 8th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Oswestry]; Occupation continued until the Roman conquest of Britain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Oswestry]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('59aaf6d8-4d0d-424b-bfba-cadddf848c1c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In one of two infilled storage pits on King John''s Hill lay a ring-headed pin fashioned from wire, in a small Hampshire hillfort of multiple defensive rings dated to approximately 100 BC.', 'Dated on ceramics to around 100 BC, this multivallate Hampshire fort was reoccupied in the 13th–14th century. Medieval pottery sits alongside Iron Age ceramics in the same excavated levels.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:59aaf6d8-4d0d-424b-bfba-cadddf848c1c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Dated on ceramics to around 100 BC, this multivallate Hampshire fort was reoccupied in the 13th–14th century. Medieval pottery sits alongside Iron Age ceramics in the same excavated levels.'' restored on King John''s Hill', 'bd3cf14d79c1b3dca234aa3add89788ee3b5919f174a7b05a8b3b46d34849334|4b52f33690d62c9c3cf797ef09eae240553eb69b735fdbd7a66454901cf988da|4b52f33690d62c9c3cf797ef09eae240553eb69b735fdbd7a66454901cf988da', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In one of two infilled storage pits on King John''s Hill lay a ring-headed pin fashioned from wire, in a small Hampshire hillfort of multiple defensive rings dated to approximately 100 BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "two infilled storage pits were found on King John''s Hill [S7]; a ring-headed pin lay in one of those storage pits [S8]; the pin was fashioned from wire [S8]; King John''s Hill is a small hillfort [S2]; it is in Hampshire [S1]; the hillfort has multiple defensive rings [S2]; it is dated to approximately 100 BC [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "King John''s Hill is a hillfort in Hampshire (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_John%27s_Hill]; The hillfort is small [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_John%27s_Hill]; The hillfort has multiple defensive rings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_John%27s_Hill]; The hillfort is dated to approximately 100 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_John%27s_Hill]; Two infilled storage pits were discovered on the hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_John%27s_Hill]; A ring-headed pin fashioned from wire was found [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_John%27s_Hill]; The pin lay in one of the two infilled storage pits [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_John%27s_Hill]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('59af6742-7a6d-45ba-9cb7-9fd0de0c51c9'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Along the Casma and Sechin rivers, the ruins of the Casma-Sechin Culture reach toward the Pacific. By 3000 BC people here were building pyramids; a plaza at Sechin Bajo dates to 3500 BCE.', 'The area of the Casma/Sechin culture extended about 40 kilometres inland from the sea. Radiocarbon dating indicates a plaza at Sechin Bajo was constructed in 3500 BCE.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:59af6742-7a6d-45ba-9cb7-9fd0de0c51c9:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The area of the Casma/Sechin culture extended about 40 kilometres inland from the sea. Radiocarbon dating indicates a plaza at Sechin Bajo was constructed in 3500 BCE.'' restored on Casma-Sechin Culture', '363d0c894523911dba60d6b93b330d461a88a428999c5710e8cfd09fab20d6ac|37b04ea95ae0f8357cf4ce209d440907d88bcdb5ac475e807d8f016ab426a539|37b04ea95ae0f8357cf4ce209d440907d88bcdb5ac475e807d8f016ab426a539', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Along the Casma and Sechin rivers, the ruins of the Casma-Sechin Culture reach toward the Pacific. By 3000 BC people here were building pyramids; a plaza at Sechin Bajo dates to 3500 BCE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the ruins of the Casma-Sechin Culture lie along the Casma and Sechin rivers [S1]; the ruins extend toward the Pacific coast [S1]; by 3000 BC people here were building pyramids [S6]; a plaza at Sechin Bajo dates to 3500 BCE [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Casma-Sechin Culture is a group of ruins (archaeological sites) in the valleys of the Casma and Sechin rivers (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casma%E2%80%93Sechin_culture]; The ruins lie along the Casma and Sechin rivers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casma%E2%80%93Sechin_culture]; The ruins reach toward the Pacific (the area extends to the Pacific coast) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casma%E2%80%93Sechin_culture]; By 3000 BC people in the Casma/Sechin valleys were building pyramids [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casma%E2%80%93Sechin_culture]; A plaza at Sechin Bajo dates to 3500 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casma%E2%80%93Sechin_culture]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5a2b16a7-5636-40c9-8875-124de3041d9b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Pacific coast, the ruins of Las Haldas cover about 40 hectares: a 370-meter long, U-shaped central area with a large mound at one end and four slightly elevated plazas.', 'The ruins at Las Haldas primarily date from the Initial Ceramic Period of 1800 BCE to 1000 BCE.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5a2b16a7-5636-40c9-8875-124de3041d9b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The ruins at Las Haldas primarily date from the Initial Ceramic Period of 1800 BCE to 1000 BCE.'' restored on Las Haldas', 'c36db5a938dcef2480f712dcb5004ae2661fdbe8f379e1cb769909255dedb82d|4c33c07c37f677e71248f4cc89622748bf26846af2ee5417f2bbd0c51f7471cc|4c33c07c37f677e71248f4cc89622748bf26846af2ee5417f2bbd0c51f7471cc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Pacific coast, the ruins of Las Haldas cover about 40 hectares: a 370-meter long, U-shaped central area with a large mound at one end and four slightly elevated plazas."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Las Haldas is on the Pacific coast [S2]; its ruins cover about 40 hectares [S4]; it has a 370-meter long central area [S4]; the central area is U-shaped [S4]; a large mound stands at one end of the central area [S4]; there are four slightly elevated plazas [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Las Haldas is an archaeological complex (ruins) on the Pacific coast (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Haldas]; The ruins of Las Haldas cover about 40 hectares [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Haldas]; The central area is 370 meters long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Haldas]; The central area is U-shaped [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Haldas]; The central area has a large mound at one end [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Haldas]; The central area has four slightly elevated plazas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Haldas]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5a440726-598a-48a2-87fc-ae833a534da6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Mammoth, horse and reindeer remains lie at Breitenbach, currently the biggest open-air settlement in western Eurasia from the time anatomically modern humans first colonized Europe.', 'The largest known open-air Aurignacian settlement site in western Eurasia, from the early Upper Palaeolithic -- when anatomically modern humans were first colonising Europe.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5a440726-598a-48a2-87fc-ae833a534da6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The largest known open-air Aurignacian settlement site in western Eurasia, from the early Upper Palaeolithic -- when anatomically modern humans were first colonising Europe.'' restored on Breitenbach - Archaeological Site', 'fceb3551f37b05b3dd5c97b446ec785f2cc4dc2c7e55a0dd6b3b97fffef253d0|8953d24dead7acedb6136563ba215ef689d708ddbf3a12028e2232b1ab155d6b|8953d24dead7acedb6136563ba215ef689d708ddbf3a12028e2232b1ab155d6b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Mammoth, horse and reindeer remains lie at Breitenbach, currently the biggest open-air settlement in western Eurasia from the time anatomically modern humans first colonized Europe."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "mammoth remains are found at Breitenbach [S7]; horse remains are found there [S8]; reindeer remains are found there [S8]; it is an open-air settlement [S1]; it dates to the time anatomically modern humans first colonized Europe [S1]; it is currently the biggest open-air settlement in western Eurasia from that time [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-033 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Breitenbach is a settlement site (near the village of Breitenbach in Saxony-Anhalt, Germany) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]; Breitenbach is an open-air settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]; Mammoth remains lie at Breitenbach [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]; Horse remains lie at Breitenbach [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]; Reindeer remains lie at Breitenbach [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]; Breitenbach is currently the biggest open-air settlement of its time period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]; The biggest-open-air-settlement superlative holds for western Eurasia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]; The settlement dates from the time anatomically modern humans first colonized Europe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitenbach_(archaeological_site)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5aaf8925-6b79-4b61-9933-be252c6f2ff6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a cliff edge in Orkney, the Tomb of the Eagles held 16,000 human bones from at least 324 people, mixed with the talons of birds, mostly white-tailed sea eagles, added at a later time.', 'A Neolithic cairn holding 16,000 bones from at least 324 people, mixed with eagle talons and skulls. Whether the eagles were totems or scavengers is still debated.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5aaf8925-6b79-4b61-9933-be252c6f2ff6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A Neolithic cairn holding 16,000 bones from at least 324 people, mixed with eagle talons and skulls. Whether the eagles were totems or scavengers is still debated.'' restored on Tomb of the Eagles', 'dd5dc68182db57f0bdaf3caa07173c55deff4e052866f354989092de47ee0cca|6b8b5be7dd53017a92f23cf574d41ddd9e939898d7437c93569b6f521eb06797|6b8b5be7dd53017a92f23cf574d41ddd9e939898d7437c93569b6f521eb06797', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a cliff edge in Orkney, the Tomb of the Eagles held 16,000 human bones from at least 324 people, mixed with the talons of birds, mostly white-tailed sea eagles, added at a later time."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Tomb of the Eagles lies on a cliff edge [S1]; it is in Orkney [S1]; 16,000 human bones were found there [S4]; the bones came from at least 324 people [S4]; the human bones were mixed with bird talons [S5]; the bird remains belong mostly to white-tailed sea eagles [S7]; the bird remains were added at a later time [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-033 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Tomb of the Eagles is a tomb [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]; The tomb stands on a cliff edge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]; The tomb is in Orkney [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]; The tomb held 16,000 human bones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]; The human bones come from at least 324 people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]; The human bones were mixed with bird talons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]; The birds were mostly white-tailed sea eagles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]; The bird remains were added at a later time than the human burials [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_the_Eagles]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5b59aada-2931-4de8-aad0-cd97a35e7e0b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Mane in Provence, the Pont sur la Laye carries its paved roadway over three limestone arches, rising sharply to the main arch, then dropping gently to the higher bank beyond.', 'A Roman bridge with a main pier protected by triangular cutwaters of rectangular stone blocks. The paved roadway rises sharply to the main arch, then drops to the far bank.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5b59aada-2931-4de8-aad0-cd97a35e7e0b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A Roman bridge with a main pier protected by triangular cutwaters of rectangular stone blocks. The paved roadway rises sharply to the main arch, then drops to the far bank.'' restored on Pont sur la Laye', 'ba869a6ba7e835efaf2e4a07642e22fbfbe617f42f3e2ffa93fbf101698e73a8|ad805f99b0c8edbe7d2a324e0f1891a91a6cf7bb14c8a1b5bdb2b76f7d6fd5c8|ad805f99b0c8edbe7d2a324e0f1891a91a6cf7bb14c8a1b5bdb2b76f7d6fd5c8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Mane in Provence, the Pont sur la Laye carries its paved roadway over three limestone arches, rising sharply to the main arch, then dropping gently to the higher bank beyond."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Pont sur la Laye is near Mane [S1]; it is in Provence [S1]; it has a paved roadway [S5]; it has three arches [S2]; it is built of limestone [S3]; the roadway rises sharply to the main arch [S5]; the roadway then drops more gently to the higher bank on the other side [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-033 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Pont sur la Laye is a bridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]; The bridge is near Mane [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]; The bridge is in Provence [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]; The bridge has three arches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]; The bridge is built of limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]; The bridge carries a paved roadway [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]; The roadway rises sharply to the main arch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]; The roadway then drops gently to the higher bank beyond [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_sur_la_Laye]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5b879809-9fb8-4e48-bd31-dbdff455de20'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Cancho Roano is the best preserved Tartessian site: a square building facing east that appears to have been ritually burned and sealed in rammed earth, no later than 370 BCE.', 'The best-preserved Tartessian site, dating to at least the 6th century BC. The building was expanded and finally deliberately burned, sealing its contents in ash.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5b879809-9fb8-4e48-bd31-dbdff455de20:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The best-preserved Tartessian site, dating to at least the 6th century BC. The building was expanded and finally deliberately burned, sealing its contents in ash.'' restored on Cancho Roano', '2bfef01e09b6b023bdf56f7f0d4b5430052ddd946f1ee2a87e47f83b4859cb67|f92d304065bfd29f5d0111454762d8650a6c1a7550037fe3d34d5d978edec28c|f92d304065bfd29f5d0111454762d8650a6c1a7550037fe3d34d5d978edec28c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cancho Roano is the best preserved Tartessian site: a square building facing east that appears to have been ritually burned and sealed in rammed earth, no later than 370 BCE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cancho Roano is the best preserved Tartessian site [S2]; its main building is square [S7]; the building faces east [S7]; the building appears to have been ritually burned [S6]; the building appears to have been sealed in rammed earth [S6]; the burning happened no later than 370 BCE [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-033 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cancho Roano is an archaeological site (a building) in Zalamea de la Serena, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]; Cancho Roano is a Tartessian site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]; Cancho Roano is the best preserved Tartessian site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]; The building is square [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]; The building faces east [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]; The building appears to have been ritually burned [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]; The building appears to have been sealed in rammed earth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]; The destruction happened no later than 370 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancho_Roano]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5be3cff9-8b07-4cfc-b667-933e2d0ae302'::uuid, 'card_description', 'South west of Vijayawada, the Undavalli Caves were carved out of solid sandstone hills; they later became a site for Buddhist monks and eventually Hindu temples.', 'A four-story rock-cut temple carved from solid sandstone, with a massive reclining Vishnu sculpted from a single granite block on the second floor.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5be3cff9-8b07-4cfc-b667-933e2d0ae302:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A four-story rock-cut temple carved from solid sandstone, with a massive reclining Vishnu sculpted from a single granite block on the second floor.'' restored on Undavalli Caves', 'bfe07580dbea9f180521b968282e385b37117d2c0c61cabb47c9040cb3efceec|2c71c293339888aa824d2a2c165b95f7e474fdc8ce398feb885de0996af5ab29|2c71c293339888aa824d2a2c165b95f7e474fdc8ce398feb885de0996af5ab29', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "South west of Vijayawada, the Undavalli Caves were carved out of solid sandstone hills; they later became a site for Buddhist monks and eventually Hindu temples."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the caves are south west of Vijayawada [S2]; they were carved out of solid sandstone hills [S3]; they later became a site for Buddhist monks [S4]; they eventually were converted into Hindu temples [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-002 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Undavalli Caves are south west of Vijayawada [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undavalli_Caves]; They were carved out of solid sandstone hills [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undavalli_Caves]; They later became a site for Buddhist monks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undavalli_Caves]; They eventually became Hindu temples [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undavalli_Caves]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5c3a138c-7052-4cb4-a482-10df27239b2e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a Cornish hill, the roughly circular hillfort of Padderbury Top keeps one surviving rampart; aerial photographs show the flat belt around it was once outer defences, reduced by ploughing.', 'A small Bronze Age defended hilltop enclosure about 73 metres across. Its single surviving rampart stands 3 metres high on the outside but less than a metre inside.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5c3a138c-7052-4cb4-a482-10df27239b2e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A small Bronze Age defended hilltop enclosure about 73 metres across. Its single surviving rampart stands 3 metres high on the outside but less than a metre inside.'' restored on Padderbury Top', '4c426067eeaa62af8d23557df8875f568c9935ca995650a4dc1df514f9668c03|f4e6e555d88c4e97f853382b30f74b777e2047831de892d5b7d8e2a3dd270379|f4e6e555d88c4e97f853382b30f74b777e2047831de892d5b7d8e2a3dd270379', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a Cornish hill, the roughly circular hillfort of Padderbury Top keeps one surviving rampart; aerial photographs show the flat belt around it was once outer defences, reduced by ploughing."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Padderbury Top is a hillfort [S1]; it sits on a hill in Cornwall [S1]; the enclosure is roughly circular [S3]; it has one surviving rampart [S5]; the rampart is surrounded by a flat belt of ground [S7]; aerial photographs show the flat belt was originally outer defences [S7]; the outer defences were reduced by ploughing [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-034 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Padderbury Top is a hillfort in Cornwall, England (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padderbury_Top]; The hillfort stands on a hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padderbury_Top]; The enclosure is roughly circular [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padderbury_Top]; The hillfort keeps one surviving rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padderbury_Top]; A belt of flat ground surrounds the rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padderbury_Top]; Aerial photographs show the flat belt was originally outer defences [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padderbury_Top]; The outer defences were reduced by ploughing [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padderbury_Top]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5cb68be0-593f-4b27-97f7-417a9314ef27'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Dzibilchaltun in Yucatán, a temple found under a later pyramid took its name from seven small effigies: the Temple of the Seven Dolls. Cenote Xlakah gave up vases and carved bones.', 'The site has been continuously occupied for thousands of years, although it has expanded into a mid-sized city and contracted back to a small town more than once in its long history.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5cb68be0-593f-4b27-97f7-417a9314ef27:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The site has been continuously occupied for thousands of years, although it has expanded into a mid-sized city and contracted back to a small town more than once in its long history.'' restored on Dzibilchaltun', '159768c0560eff34b478410a92a02501c650655a8d6e8cda2097116360f2553a|fd17cea83f5b6d811077880e2903dcc06f19488bd14f118a186c20e4dac24c46|fd17cea83f5b6d811077880e2903dcc06f19488bd14f118a186c20e4dac24c46', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Dzibilchaltun in Yucatán, a temple found under a later pyramid took its name from seven small effigies: the Temple of the Seven Dolls. Cenote Xlakah gave up vases and carved bones."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dzibilchaltun is in Yucatan [S1]; a temple there was discovered under the ruins of a later temple pyramid [S4]; the temple was named after seven small effigies found at the site [S4]; the temple is called the Temple of the Seven Dolls [S4]; the site has a cenote named Cenote Xlakah [S5]; vases were found in the cenote [S7]; carved bones were found in the cenote [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-034 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dzibilchaltun is a Maya archaeological site in the state of Yucatán, Mexico (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzibilchaltun]; A temple was found under a later pyramid (the temple was discovered under the ruins of a later temple pyramid) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzibilchaltun]; The temple is called the Temple of the Seven Dolls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzibilchaltun]; The temple took its name from seven small effigies [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzibilchaltun]; The site has a cenote named Cenote Xlakah [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzibilchaltun]; Cenote Xlakah gave up vases [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzibilchaltun]; Cenote Xlakah gave up carved bones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzibilchaltun]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5d97461b-02b7-4227-8918-bc6c80a531ac'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the Mashavera valley, Dmanisi gave up human fossils 1.8 million years old, the earliest known human presence in the Caucasus, and skulls suggesting many Homo species were one lineage.', 'Hominin remains dating to 1.8 million years ago make this the earliest known evidence of human ancestors outside Africa. Five skulls found here show surprising anatomical variety.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5d97461b-02b7-4227-8918-bc6c80a531ac:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Hominin remains dating to 1.8 million years ago make this the earliest known evidence of human ancestors outside Africa. Five skulls found here show surprising anatomical variety.'' restored on Dmanisi', 'c4834fd51474b2b9699e0bd50ec5d02492bb163dd5668eaf9872ea1539330614|60f59b97cdff5e8720c2c68bd3c7ec06a70632c91e012b2e856ff28f0d82ecd4|60f59b97cdff5e8720c2c68bd3c7ec06a70632c91e012b2e856ff28f0d82ecd4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Mashavera valley, Dmanisi gave up human fossils 1.8 million years old, the earliest known human presence in the Caucasus, and skulls suggesting many Homo species were one lineage."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dmanisi is in the river valley of Mashavera [S1]; early human fossils were found at Dmanisi [S6]; the fossils are 1.8 million years old [S7]; the fossils represent the earliest known human presence in the Caucasus [S8]; skulls found there led to the hypothesis that many separate Homo species were a single lineage [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-034 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dmanisi is an archaeological site in the river valley of the Mashavera, Georgia (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmanisi]; Dmanisi gave up human (hominin) fossils [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmanisi]; The human fossils are 1.8 million years old [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmanisi]; The fossils are the earliest known human presence in the Caucasus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmanisi]; Skulls found at Dmanisi suggest that many Homo species were a single lineage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmanisi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5e5354ff-e651-496a-a9bb-cf779d3cc501'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Above where the River Dee meets the River Alwen, Caer Drewyn began as a small enclosure against a rock outcrop; a later fortress was ringed by a dry-stone wall instead of earthen ramparts.', 'Early hillfort north of Corwen with a large stone rampart, a guard chamber in the north-east entrance, and a deep defensive ditch. The westward and northward entrances remain visible.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5e5354ff-e651-496a-a9bb-cf779d3cc501:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Early hillfort north of Corwen with a large stone rampart, a guard chamber in the north-east entrance, and a deep defensive ditch. The westward and northward entrances remain visible.'' restored on Caer Drewyn', 'b873f10190e60307988510aedfc2d7e103272fd16015fabf2efb63cff0b9d36e|2a509f0860df56f7effc8ab90026d2d31de5aa3e6001d8954cefb9838ea4a6bf|2a509f0860df56f7effc8ab90026d2d31de5aa3e6001d8954cefb9838ea4a6bf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Above where the River Dee meets the River Alwen, Caer Drewyn began as a small enclosure against a rock outcrop; a later fortress was ringed by a dry-stone wall instead of earthen ramparts."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Caer Drewyn sits on a hill spur above the confluence of the River Dee and the River Alwen [S3]; the earliest structure was a small enclosure [S4]; that enclosure was built against a natural rock outcrop [S4]; a later fortress was surrounded by a dry-stone wall [S5]; the later fortress lacks the earthen ramparts of other local hillforts [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-034 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Caer Drewyn is a hillfort near Corwen, Denbighshire, Wales (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Drewyn]; It lies above the confluence of the River Dee and the River Alwen [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Drewyn]; Caer Drewyn began as a small enclosure built against a rock outcrop [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Drewyn]; A later fortress was ringed by a dry-stone wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Drewyn]; The fortress has a stone wall instead of earthen ramparts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Drewyn]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5e73f7ad-ee05-4eaa-bea0-bb66eb0abe9d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'After the earthquake of 62 AD, parts of the House of Julia Felix in Pompeii became rental apartments and a grotto of nymphs with a water-stair fountain was added. Frescoes show merchants.', 'After the 62 AD earthquake damaged Pompeii, the owner converted this grand estate into rental apartments and a public bath. Buried by Vesuvius 17 years later.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:5e73f7ad-ee05-4eaa-bea0-bb66eb0abe9d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''After the 62 AD earthquake damaged Pompeii, the owner converted this grand estate into rental apartments and a public bath. Buried by Vesuvius 17 years later.'' restored on House of Julia Felix, Pompeii', '1de045fd35a65d31278b2b89d2d21dd1b40ca43c16c2dba3d4ccef4de69d24e2|1b3d035a84999401e56cc20b3bca65d2d968a5e98c86f04c09419b8d3441d8b0|1b3d035a84999401e56cc20b3bca65d2d968a5e98c86f04c09419b8d3441d8b0', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "After the earthquake of 62 AD, parts of the House of Julia Felix in Pompeii became rental apartments and a grotto of nymphs with a water-stair fountain was added. Frescoes show merchants."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the House of Julia Felix is in Pompeii [S1]; after the earthquake of 62 AD, parts of it were converted to rental apartments [S2]; a grotto of nymphs with a water-stair fountain was added as a modification after the earthquake of 62 AD [S6]; frescoes in the house depict merchants [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-034 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The House of Julia Felix is a large Roman property (praedia) in the city of Pompeii, Italy (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Julia_Felix]; After the earthquake of 62 AD, parts of the house became rental apartments [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Julia_Felix]; A grotto of nymphs with a water-stair fountain was added after the earthquake of 62 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Julia_Felix]; Frescoes in the house show merchants [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Julia_Felix]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('60ae4c7a-9c8e-49cf-b85b-d49d8022787c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near the summit of Mount Hermon, the two Roman temples of Qasr Chbib have northern walls carved out of solid bedrock, a design Kevin Butcher suggested brought them closer to the gods.', 'Two Roman temples near the summit of Mount Hermon, with walls carved directly out of solid bedrock. A contested border dispute between neighbouring states has left the site poorly documented.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:60ae4c7a-9c8e-49cf-b85b-d49d8022787c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Two Roman temples near the summit of Mount Hermon, with walls carved directly out of solid bedrock. A contested border dispute between neighbouring states has left the site poorly documented.'' restored on Qasr Chbib', '7c8a96df2847e692a39cf791731ef2dc1a72a609cb18f0e10f14d78b7f406c28|d7c2fe97ebe72b8228c9a68625b8068dfcd98b9982a58cc789d37fbf93cd86fe|d7c2fe97ebe72b8228c9a68625b8068dfcd98b9982a58cc789d37fbf93cd86fe', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near the summit of Mount Hermon, the two Roman temples of Qasr Chbib have northern walls carved out of solid bedrock, a design Kevin Butcher suggested brought them closer to the gods."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Qasr Chbib lies near the summit of Mount Hermon [S1]; Qasr Chbib consists of two Roman temples [S1]; both temples have northern walls carved out of solid bedrock [S2]; Kevin Butcher suggested this rock-cut design was meant to bring the temples closer to the gods [S2, S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Qasr Chbib is a complex of two Roman temples (the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qasr_Chbib]; The temples lie near the summit of Mount Hermon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qasr_Chbib]; The northern walls of both temples are carved out of solid bedrock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qasr_Chbib]; Kevin Butcher suggested this design was meant to bring the temples closer to the gods [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qasr_Chbib]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('612ff287-3919-4ae9-bb6b-50ea7548b64b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On top of a Peruvian mountain of the same name, the ruins of Huacramarca sit at 4,150 metres, their name possibly drawn from the Quechua words for horn and village.', 'Mountain ruins at 4,150 metres elevation, one of countless unnamed Andean sites still waiting for systematic study. Its builders and purpose remain undocumented.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:612ff287-3919-4ae9-bb6b-50ea7548b64b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Mountain ruins at 4,150 metres elevation, one of countless unnamed Andean sites still waiting for systematic study. Its builders and purpose remain undocumented.'' restored on Huacramarca', '9a99a98251e476ff4442b626a923451cdb2b4aa27df625909f96cfe9f4457a61|e320333b43e7105c05d279f72964d6b19a1035beac87b675cf6cb397ef399d9f|e320333b43e7105c05d279f72964d6b19a1035beac87b675cf6cb397ef399d9f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On top of a Peruvian mountain of the same name, the ruins of Huacramarca sit at 4,150 metres, their name possibly drawn from the Quechua words for horn and village."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huacramarca is on a mountain of the same name in Peru [S1]; the ruins sit on top of the mountain [S3]; the ruins are at an elevation of 4,150 metres [S3]; the name possibly derives from the Quechua words for horn and village [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huacramarca is an archaeological site (ruins) on a mountain of the same name in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huacramarca]; The ruins sit on top of the mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huacramarca]; The ruins are at an elevation of 4,150 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huacramarca]; The name possibly comes from the Quechua words for horn (waqra) and village (marka) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huacramarca]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6148883a-659c-40c4-bcf4-ef086f860f40'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Waddon Hill in Dorset, its name Old English for wheat hill, the Second Legion under Vespasian built a short-lived Roman fort on a narrow ridge with steep natural slopes.', 'Positioned on a narrow ridge at 210 meters near Beaminster, with steep natural slopes for defense. The Wessex Ridgeway passes just north of the summit, placing it on a major ancient route.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:6148883a-659c-40c4-bcf4-ef086f860f40:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Positioned on a narrow ridge at 210 meters near Beaminster, with steep natural slopes for defense. The Wessex Ridgeway passes just north of the summit, placing it on a major ancient route.'' restored on Waddon Hill', '1b5c776bc6c8d282df0562842c3a3b61df84de85eefa4c349b33578890073543|fe5d7f47ac078a9b37458d659562eb356008287704f56ba8410f3abc7e90c3f6|fe5d7f47ac078a9b37458d659562eb356008287704f56ba8410f3abc7e90c3f6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Waddon Hill in Dorset, its name Old English for wheat hill, the Second Legion under Vespasian built a short-lived Roman fort on a narrow ridge with steep natural slopes."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Waddon Hill is in Dorset [S1]; the name Waddon is Old English for wheat hill [S2]; the fort was built by the Second Legion under Vespasian [S4]; Waddon Hill is the site of a short-lived Roman fort [S1]; the fort is on a narrow ridge [S3]; the ridge has steep natural slopes [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Waddon Hill in Dorset is the site of a Roman fort (the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waddon_Hill]; The Roman fort was short-lived [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waddon_Hill]; The name Waddon is Old English for wheat hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waddon_Hill]; The fort was built by the Second Legion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waddon_Hill]; The Second Legion that built it was under Vespasian [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waddon_Hill]; The fort stands on a narrow ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waddon_Hill]; The ridge has steep natural slopes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waddon_Hill]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('616719a2-c7ee-4162-b6f6-da52b85dbc4a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Where the Oude Rijn met Corbulo''s newly dug canal, Roman Matilo guarded the frontier of Germania Inferior; the fort itself seems to date from around 70 AD, perhaps after a small watchtower.', 'A Roman castellum on the southern bank of the Oude Rijn, guarding the Limes Germanicus. No pre-Roman finds have appeared here — the garrison was the first human settlement on this spot.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:616719a2-c7ee-4162-b6f6-da52b85dbc4a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A Roman castellum on the southern bank of the Oude Rijn, guarding the Limes Germanicus. No pre-Roman finds have appeared here — the garrison was the first human settlement on this spot.'' restored on Matilo', '71cf4454036fb77e066fa73b78521a3dbe22c8d35909dd5031ff0b5f631db5c5|b8303d7d9fb845847f3642a52c627b93e38b274bfab48ee0c55e7ab932c80539|b8303d7d9fb845847f3642a52c627b93e38b274bfab48ee0c55e7ab932c80539', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Where the Oude Rijn met Corbulo''s newly dug canal, Roman Matilo guarded the frontier of Germania Inferior; the fort itself seems to date from around 70 AD, perhaps after a small watchtower."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Matilo stood at the meeting place of the Oude Rijn and the newly dug canal [S6]; the canal was Corbulo''s canal [S5]; Matilo was a Roman fort [S1]; it protected the Roman borders in the province of Germania Inferior [S2]; the evidence seems to suggest the fort itself was built around 70 AD [S7]; a small watchtower might have preceded the fort [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Matilo was a Roman fort (castellum) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilo]; Matilo guarded the frontier of Germania Inferior [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilo]; Matilo stood where the Oude Rijn met the newly dug canal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilo]; The canal was Corbulo''s canal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilo]; The fort itself seems to date from around 70 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilo]; Perhaps a small watchtower preceded the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilo]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('64b2e0cd-cd66-4612-802e-d0789d8f8b14'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Kandahar, the tell of Said Qala Tepe, about 8 meters high, held six layers, from an intensive prehistoric settlement attributed to the Helmand culture to a Kushan–Sassanid cemetery.', '4th–3rd millennium BC tell near Kandahar, 200 metres square and 8 metres high. Dense Helmand culture Bronze Age evidence — one of the best-documented prehistoric sites in the Kandahar Plain.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:64b2e0cd-cd66-4612-802e-d0789d8f8b14:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''4th–3rd millennium BC tell near Kandahar, 200 metres square and 8 metres high. Dense Helmand culture Bronze Age evidence — one of the best-documented prehistoric sites in the Kandahar Plain.'' restored on Said Qala Tepe', '9df168c51e6dc412ebdd37a589a10d57c1b7ceff03b78e008a3464b0440ea946|161cc2d643438a291703bb94aea8ddabf76fcfd3d969acdd1892db59a2a770bf|161cc2d643438a291703bb94aea8ddabf76fcfd3d969acdd1892db59a2a770bf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Kandahar, the tell of Said Qala Tepe, about 8 meters high, held six layers, from an intensive prehistoric settlement attributed to the Helmand culture to a Kushan–Sassanid cemetery."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Said Qala Tepe is near Kandahar [S1]; the site is a tell [S2]; the tell is about 8 meters high [S2]; six layers were observed during the excavation [S5]; the site shows an intensive prehistoric settlement [S3]; the prehistoric settlement is attributed to the Helmand culture [S3]; one of the layers is a cemetery of the Kushan-Sassanid period [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Said Qala Tepe is a tell (archaeological site) in Afghanistan (the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]; The site is near Kandahar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]; The tell is about 8 meters high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]; Six layers were observed in the excavation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]; The site has an intensive prehistoric settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]; The prehistoric settlement is attributed to the Helmand culture [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]; One layer is a cemetery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]; The cemetery dates to the Kushan-Sassanid period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Said_Qala_Tepe]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6645e3f5-bc18-46f5-854c-5b89dd96650e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Suffield Block in Alberta lies the British Block Cairn, dating from around 1400 CE: a large boulder cairn surrounded by a ring of stones and a human effigy figure.', 'A large boulder cairn surrounded by a stone ring and a human effigy figure, dating to about 1400 AD. The effigy is one of few such figures found on the northern plains.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:6645e3f5-bc18-46f5-854c-5b89dd96650e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''A large boulder cairn surrounded by a stone ring and a human effigy figure, dating to about 1400 AD. The effigy is one of few such figures found on the northern plains.'' restored on British Block Cairn', '35433cf7490592c62e9989e8f19aa5d4ae8e7c4a065d014c6f55ba8bc5fdfac4|115738b6a6d5302c4fcfdc62f9df7a81261be43a3e8d79cf71c03adf06ff2a8e|115738b6a6d5302c4fcfdc62f9df7a81261be43a3e8d79cf71c03adf06ff2a8e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Suffield Block in Alberta lies the British Block Cairn, dating from around 1400 CE: a large boulder cairn surrounded by a ring of stones and a human effigy figure."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the British Block Cairn is on the Suffield Block in Alberta [S1]; the site dates from around 1400 CE [S3]; the site consists of a large boulder cairn [S3]; the cairn is surrounded by a ring of stones [S3]; the site includes a human effigy figure [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "British Block Cairn is a boulder cairn (a cairn site) in Canada [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Block_Cairn]; It lies on the Suffield Block in Alberta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Block_Cairn]; It dates from around 1400 CE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Block_Cairn]; The cairn is large and surrounded by a ring of stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Block_Cairn]; The site includes a human effigy figure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Block_Cairn]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('66b6fcb0-605a-4988-9c26-50d92bdb45dd'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near St Buryan, the Bronze Age circle of Boscawen-Un holds 19 stones, 18 of grey granite and one of bright quartz, around a leaning middle stone. A west-facing gap may have been an entrance.', '19 upright stones set in an ellipse 24.9 by 21.9 metres, with a leaning central stone just south of centre. A west-facing gap in the ring may mark a deliberate entrance to this Cornwall circle.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:66b6fcb0-605a-4988-9c26-50d92bdb45dd:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''19 upright stones set in an ellipse 24.9 by 21.9 metres, with a leaning central stone just south of centre. A west-facing gap in the ring may mark a deliberate entrance to this Cornwall circle.'' restored on Boscawen-Un', '12de5ab34992b4f906ec0a8c4ec8352ef9902eb79721ba87f2145a6c7bef34fa|8ed0583c8b1224e79e84a1c164d7915fe6a40ddf8ae2009937a5aef8478ce71c|8ed0583c8b1224e79e84a1c164d7915fe6a40ddf8ae2009937a5aef8478ce71c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near St Buryan, the Bronze Age circle of Boscawen-Un holds 19 stones, 18 of grey granite and one of bright quartz, around a leaning middle stone. A west-facing gap may have been an entrance."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the circle is near St Buryan [S1]; it is a Bronze Age circle [S1]; it holds 19 stones [S2, S5]; 18 of the stones are grey granite and one is bright quartz [S5]; the stones surround a leaning middle stone [S2]; a west-facing gap exists in the circle [S3]; the gap may have been an entrance (hedged) [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-003 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Boscawen-Un is a Bronze Age stone circle near St Buryan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boscawen-Un]; The site is in Cornwall, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boscawen-Un]; The circle holds 19 stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boscawen-Un]; 18 of the stones are grey granite [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boscawen-Un]; One stone is bright quartz [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boscawen-Un]; A leaning stone stands in the middle of the circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boscawen-Un]; There is a west-facing gap that may have been an entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boscawen-Un]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('672f18a4-e818-4367-b593-fe0432b134a7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Laguna de las Momias, six mausoleums built against the rock held approximately two hundred funeral deposits, each a seated mummy in textiles, in caves painted with indecipherable symbols.', 'Around 900 AD, the Chachapoyas placed mummies in cliff-side tombs above a high-altitude lake. Laguna de las Momias yielded extraordinary burials; condors overhead gave this Amazonian site its names.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:672f18a4-e818-4367-b593-fe0432b134a7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Around 900 AD, the Chachapoyas placed mummies in cliff-side tombs above a high-altitude lake. Laguna de las Momias yielded extraordinary burials; condors overhead gave this Amazonian site its names.'' restored on Laguna de las Momias', '5519c75e32fb83defa59a60772d09daa83ea070d24d7f35629762301b5cedb9e|6582b95ea2f8a70637c8d9b7699b488139e9724172ac0af24a5de284b7ccdb6e|6582b95ea2f8a70637c8d9b7699b488139e9724172ac0af24a5de284b7ccdb6e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Laguna de las Momias, six mausoleums built against the rock held approximately two hundred funeral deposits, each a seated mummy in textiles, in caves painted with indecipherable symbols."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "six mausoleums were found at Laguna de las Momias [S3]; the enclosures were built against the rock, with the rock as their fourth wall [S5]; the mausoleums held approximately two hundred funeral deposits [S7]; each funeral deposit contains a mummy in a seated position [S2]; the mummies are wrapped in textiles [S2]; the tombs were housed in caves [S4]; the caves have paintings on the walls [S4]; the paintings contain indecipherable symbols [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Laguna de las Momias is a burial site of mausoleums [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]; There are six mausoleums [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]; The mausoleums were built against the rock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]; They held approximately two hundred funeral deposits [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]; Each deposit is a mummy in a seated position [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]; The mummies are wrapped in textiles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]; The tombs are in caves with painted walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]; The painted symbols are indecipherable [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_de_las_Momias]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('67568bad-9a47-45b1-bae3-002ad8b28545'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Greek colony in the 4th century BC, an ally of Rome in the Punic Wars, then Roman Dianium, where Quintus Sertorius set up a naval base: Dénia carries the layers of many civilizations.', 'Settled from the 4th century BC as a coastal trading post with pre-Roman, Greek, Roman, Islamic, and Napoleonic layers. Significant ruins still visible on the hillsides outside the town.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:67568bad-9a47-45b1-bae3-002ad8b28545:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''Settled from the 4th century BC as a coastal trading post with pre-Roman, Greek, Roman, Islamic, and Napoleonic layers. Significant ruins still visible on the hillsides outside the town.'' restored on Dénia', 'f9f7395d03c1b546a20e2acef6f668d07cb36cad8bcb2f4661f5c8e9890d0fa5|612b3b6903cc3b2128c1ca5f35ca7b4598dfa77051abb981e5748e92f98ff220|612b3b6903cc3b2128c1ca5f35ca7b4598dfa77051abb981e5748e92f98ff220', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A Greek colony in the 4th century BC, an ally of Rome in the Punic Wars, then Roman Dianium, where Quintus Sertorius set up a naval base: Dénia carries the layers of many civilizations."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Denia was a Greek colony in the 4th century BC [S2]; it was an ally of Rome during the Punic Wars [S3]; it was later Roman, under the name Dianium [S3]; Quintus Sertorius established a naval base there [S4]; Denia''s heritage was shaped by many civilizations [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Denia is a historic coastal city in Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9nia]; It was a Greek colony in the 4th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9nia]; It was an ally of Rome in the Punic Wars [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9nia]; It became Roman Dianium [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9nia]; Quintus Sertorius set up a naval base there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9nia]; It carries the layers of many civilizations [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9nia]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('67661f87-a03e-4e21-b8d1-c62fbf91361d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A passage 36 m long leads through a deep doorway into the Treasury of Atreus: a burial chamber under a corbelled dome, the largest of the known Mycenaean tholos tombs.', 'The Treasury of Atreus is the largest and most elaborate of the known Mycenaean tholos tombs.', 'teaser-card-s023-rollback:67661f87-a03e-4e21-b8d1-c62fbf91361d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s023: card_description ''The Treasury of Atreus is the largest and most elaborate of the known Mycenaean tholos tombs.'' restored on Treasury of Atreus', '0e0d4893f5ad7a68eda1163db0129ec5d7472eb3cc14e3ec239ba2096888853e|7cf181b758f389698507a49156b4cb46023ffb88807180b828d8c8d764e8c02c|7cf181b758f389698507a49156b4cb46023ffb88807180b828d8c8d764e8c02c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-013", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A passage 36 m long leads through a deep doorway into the Treasury of Atreus: a burial chamber under a corbelled dome, the largest of the known Mycenaean tholos tombs."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-013 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a passage (dromos) leads to the tomb [S2]; the passage is 36 m long [S4]; the passage leads through a deep doorway [S2]; the doorway leads into a burial chamber [S2]; the burial chamber is under a corbelled dome [S2]; it is a Mycenaean tholos tomb [S1]; it is the largest of the known Mycenaean tholos tombs [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Treasury of Atreus is a tomb (burial chamber) at Mycenae [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury_of_Atreus]; It is a Mycenaean th